WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.850
Danielle: So this is gonna be a live recording.

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:05.290
Well, not a live...A recording of our podcast,
before a live audience.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.669
These are the interns, right, of The American
Enterprise Institute?

00:00:08.669 --> 00:00:11.129
Christina: Interns, and senior staff, and...president...

00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:15.660
Danielle: We didn't actually invite them,
but they heard you were here, and in their

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.310
way they just all showed up.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.439
Christina: We ended up in this room.

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:19.939
We were gonna be at our dark studio.

00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:20.939
Jordan: Awesome.

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:21.939
No, well this is nice.

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:22.939
It's good to have...

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:25.119
It's nice to be in front of all you people,
so...hypothetically.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:26.730
We'll see how civilized you are.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:27.730
Christina: Yes.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:28.730
Jordan: And all of that.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:29.730
Christina: Is Antifa here?

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:30.730
No Antifa?

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:31.730
Okay.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:32.730
Jordan: No, it's good.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:33.730
Good.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:34.730
Danielle: All right.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:35.730
So, we ready?

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.730
Christina: Yeah.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:37.730
Jordan: It's really nice to see you, by the
way.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:38.730
Christina: It's so nice to see you.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.730
Okay, start.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:40.730
Go.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:41.730
Danielle: Welcome to "The Femsplainers."

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:42.730
I'm Danielle Crittenden.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.800
Christina: And I'm Christina Hoff Sommers,
and we are thrilled to have the father of

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.830
all mansplainers in our studio today.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:53.370
Danielle: Chris, you know, I think he's actually
more of a man whisperer.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:01:00.170
Christina: Yes, a man whisperer, the mad genius
behind the intellectual dark web, whatever.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.990
Welcome to "The Femsplainers," Jordan Peterson.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:03.620
Jordan: Thank you very much.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.520
Christina: So delighted to have you, and it's
just...

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:07.549
Danielle: It's an honor to have you.

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:08.549
Christina: ...an honor.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:09.549
It's amazing.

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:12.590
Danielle: And a note to our listeners, we're
recording this in front of a live audience

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:18.859
at the American Enterprise Institute in Washington,
D.C., where Christina is a resident scholar.

00:01:18.859 --> 00:01:23.680
And there'll be video of the podcast as well,
and we will let you know where to find that,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:24.799
when it's ready.

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:29.799
And we're also grateful to AEI every week
for the use of its recording studio for the

00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:30.799
podcast.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:35.250
Christina: And now for an introduction, though
he needs no introduction to the people here.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:40.130
But, Jordan Peterson is a professor at the
University of Toronto, and author of many

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:44.159
books, and poster of many fantastic lecturers.

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:49.810
His most recent book has...I can't keep track
of how many languages it's been translated

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:54.060
into, and the sales, just a phenomenally successful
book tour.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.820
In fact, my first question is really about
your tour.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.999
You look pretty good for somebody who's visited,
what, 100 cities in the past year?

00:02:03.999 --> 00:02:04.999
Jordan: Mm-hmm.

00:02:04.999 --> 00:02:05.999
Since January 23rd.

00:02:05.999 --> 00:02:07.619
Christina: I don't know how you do it.

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:08.619
And...

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:09.619
Jordan: Mostly flying.

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:12.470
Christina: Well, what do you do for fun?

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:13.580
Do you ever get to relax

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.320
Jordan: In brief moments.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.570
Christina: And what do you do?

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.910
Go on Twitter and get...

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.640
Jordan: Oh, God yes, although I wouldn't qualify
that as relaxing.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.940
And I try to, you know, forestall that temptation
as much as possible.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.090
Well, I have the odd amount, bit of time that
I can spend with my wife.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:35.160
She does travel with me, and so, you know,
we've had...we try to take some time to walk

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.110
around the cities that we're in, and see what
we can.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:43.420
We're usually not at any given place for more
than a day or two, and they're usually pretty

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:49.550
packed up with...well, whatever is associated
with the lecture, and then with press, that

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:50.890
the publishers usually arrange.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.460
Christina: Oh, I heard you interviewed in
Sweden, you were in Stockholm.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:59.120
And you had a half an hour to visit the city
with your wife.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.120
Jordan: Yep.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.120
Yeah.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.260
Christina: And you loved it, but that...you
know, it's very tiring.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:03.260
Jordan: Yeah.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.230
Well, you take your breaks where you get them.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:10.250
Well, the thing is is that the lecture tour
is unbelievably positive.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.750
And a lot of this is ridiculously positive,
you know?

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:19.570
Like, so if I'm going out on the streets now,
or in cafes, or, you know, airports, I meet

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:24.620
people all the time, and they're always polite,
and they're always happy to see me.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:29.600
And they always have some very touching story
to relate.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:36.360
And then the audience's themselves are very
positively predisposed to whatever it is that

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.360
we're doing together.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:44.541
And so that makes it a lot easier to stay
motivated, and to continue

00:03:44.541 --> 00:03:45.541
Christina: Right.

00:03:45.541 --> 00:03:50.240
Jordan: You know, I mean, it's demanding,
because everything's scheduled so tightly,

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.160
and I do a different lecture every night,
every time I go...

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:56.160
Christina: I know.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.730
I find that amazing, because I give a lot
of lectures that I anguish over every word,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:03.080
and then I have another one...

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:04.700
And you go up without notes.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:05.700
Jordan: Yeah.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.440
Well, I have a large collection of, you know,
things that I know how to talk about, and

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:15.070
usually what I try to do is to formulate a
problem before the lecture.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:21.030
So I'm addressing a specific problem, and
then I can track how I would set up the argument,

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.040
and then I walk through it.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.580
But part of it's also an attempt to formulate
the argument on the fly, you know, to make

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.180
the question...what would you say, to formulate
it more precisely, and to make a more precise

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:32.180
and engaging answer.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:36.430
And then I can use the audience to judge whether
or not that's happening.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:41.629
And so it's also a real challenge to do that,
so I enjoy that.

00:04:41.629 --> 00:04:45.530
And it's an excellent intellectual workout.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:52.070
And I've been recording the lectures, and
I've been using some of them to write the

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:55.620
first draft of the chapters for my next book,
and for books after that.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:00.979
And so, you know, I'm able to maximize the,
what would you say, the utility of doing this

00:05:00.979 --> 00:05:02.419
at each event.

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:06.949
And my wife seems to be particularly well
suited to traveling like that.

00:05:06.949 --> 00:05:12.850
She actually enjoys it quite a bit, and is
a very stable person.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:19.590
And so that's also helpful, and, you know,
it's nice to have an extra brain along, because

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.030
things are scheduled so tightly that we don't
ever have any room for error.

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.430
Danielle: Yes.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:27.169
Well, we're going to...

00:05:27.169 --> 00:05:31.560
I don't know how intellectually rigorous we
plan to be with you today, because we know

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.909
that whenever you're on one of these platforms,
you're talking about your ideas.

00:05:35.909 --> 00:05:39.750
But on "The Femsplainers," we wanna hear a
little bit more about Jordan Peterson.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.909
Desperately and definitely wanna hear about
your wife, Tammy.

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:44.969
Jordan: Yep.

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:51.990
Danielle: And also, you're so well known for
your views on men, or how your ideas have

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:56.580
been taken up so enthusiastic by young men,
but we wanna talk to you about women.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:57.800
Jordan: Yep, that's good.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:04.099
Danielle: So, but one of the things you and
I share is that we both grew up in Canada.

00:06:04.099 --> 00:06:09.669
I promised Christine I would not do my Canadian
accent while you were here.

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:14.199
But you grew up in rural Alberta, I grew up
in Toronto.

00:06:14.199 --> 00:06:23.039
And you are, what, the country's most famous
guru now, since Marshall McLuhan.

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:28.740
But does the fact that you came from Canada
have any effect on your views, do you think?

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.449
Has it formed you in any way?

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:35.650
I mean, would it be the same if, you think,
you'd grown up in rural Texas?

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:38.120
How has Canada contributed to your worldview?

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:42.349
Christina: She's always looking to promote
Canada, so go for it.

00:06:42.349 --> 00:06:43.740
Danielle: For the Canadian angle.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.680
Hey, we have listeners in Canada.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:51.460
Jordan: Well, I think the particular part
of Canada I grew up in probably was formative,

00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:52.460
to some degree.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:58.169
I mean, the town I grew up in was only 50
years old, you know, and the particular part

00:06:58.169 --> 00:07:02.860
of the world that I grew up in was really
the last settled part of the North American

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:03.860
prairie.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:04.860
Danielle: This was outside of Edmonton, correct?

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.610
Jordan: Yeah, about 400 miles north of Edmonton.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:07.610
Christina: Oh, 400 miles?

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:08.819
Jordan: Yeah, yeah, it's right at the tip
of the...

00:07:08.819 --> 00:07:09.819
Danielle: That's a short distance.

00:07:09.819 --> 00:07:10.849
Christina: A short distance?

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:11.849
Jordan: Yeah.

00:07:11.849 --> 00:07:12.849
Christina: A suburb.

00:07:12.849 --> 00:07:16.639
Jordan: So, the prairie stretches up that
far north, it stretches up farther north in

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:19.349
Alberta than it does anywhere else in the
North American continent.

00:07:19.349 --> 00:07:23.719
And so we were at the tip of viable farming,
essentially.

00:07:23.719 --> 00:07:29.059
And so it was a new place, and it was a rather
raw place.

00:07:29.059 --> 00:07:34.479
And it was a rather harsh place, in many ways,
especially because of the winter.

00:07:34.479 --> 00:07:38.800
And it was fundamentally a working-class place,
although a prosperous working class place,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:39.800
right?

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.759
Because most of the industry there was related
to the oil and gas industry, and although

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:49.900
it was cyclical, when things were good, working-class
people could make a very good living.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:51.880
Danielle: This is during the '70s, so through
the whole...

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:52.880
Jordan: Yeah.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:53.880
Yeah, that's right.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:57.580
Christina: Was it fun to be a kid in...400
miles outside a small town?

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.719
Jordan: Yeah, I liked it when I was a kid.

00:08:00.719 --> 00:08:04.009
I wouldn't say it was as fun when I was a
teenager.

00:08:04.009 --> 00:08:05.009
Danielle: Right.

00:08:05.009 --> 00:08:10.879
Jordan: But I'm not convinced that, you know,
the majority of people who are teenagers necessarily

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:13.529
have the most wonderful time of it.

00:08:13.529 --> 00:08:18.220
I think adults often look backwards at the
past through rose-colored glasses.

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:23.479
I think that's what the cartoonist Trudeau
accused Reagan of doing, continually.

00:08:23.479 --> 00:08:24.580
Garry Trudeau.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:27.189
Christina: Here at the American Institute! Don't insult Mr. Reagan.

00:08:27.189 --> 00:08:28.189
Jordan: No, no.

00:08:28.189 --> 00:08:29.189
No, no.

00:08:29.189 --> 00:08:30.189
Christina: I'm kidding, I'm kidding.

00:08:30.189 --> 00:08:31.189
Jordan: I'm definitely not.

00:08:31.189 --> 00:08:35.030
Danielle: I think the words you used for it
in your book was teenage wasteland, I think

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:36.209
is what you called it.

00:08:36.209 --> 00:08:37.209
Jordan: Yeah, yeah.

00:08:37.209 --> 00:08:43.510
Danielle: But its Canadian-ness, how does
that formed you, or affected you, if at all?

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:44.510
Maybe it didn't.

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:46.110
Jordan: It's hard to say.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:51.220
I mean, I've lived in lots of different parts
of Canada, now, and Canada is quite different.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:55.709
I lived in, well, Alberta for a while, and
it had this particular flavor of existence.

00:08:55.709 --> 00:09:03.420
I mean, mostly in Fairview I was striving
to leave and to move ahead, let's say, or

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:08.240
to move...I hesitate to say up, but somewhere
different, somewhere more urban.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.980
But that's the case with many people.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:14.170
I mean, the small towns all across the west
in the U.S. and in Canada are dying.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.470
I mean, they're down to nothing, because everyone's
moved to the cities.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:22.670
I lived in Montreal for a good while, and
that was interesting because it was a very,

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:23.899
very different culture.

00:09:23.899 --> 00:09:28.820
It was a culture that was, to some degree,
stratified by language and by class.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:32.589
And none of that was true in Alberta, because
it was so new that there's no class structure.

00:09:32.589 --> 00:09:34.060
So that was quite interesting,

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:35.930
Danielle: Right, and you worked...

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:40.279
What I loved...I pulled a passage, because
I think, as you say, people are born in small

00:09:40.279 --> 00:09:43.079
places everywhere, and some wanna leave and
some don't.

00:09:43.079 --> 00:09:45.649
You said, "I wanted to be elsewhere.

00:09:45.649 --> 00:09:47.630
I wasn't the only one.

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:50.820
Everyone who eventually left the Fairview
I grew up, knew they were leaving by the age

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:51.820
of 12.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:55.529
I knew, and my wife, who grew up with me on
the same street, knew."

00:09:55.529 --> 00:09:56.529
Jordan: Mm-hmm.

00:09:56.529 --> 00:09:57.920
Danielle: What was that thing?

00:09:57.920 --> 00:09:58.920
What would you call that?

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:01.870
What's the thing that makes you wanna leave,
and sets you off?

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:06.649
Because as you point out, there was no class
system, education was cheap in Canada, compared

00:10:06.649 --> 00:10:07.649
to the United States...

00:10:07.649 --> 00:10:08.649
Jordan: Yep.

00:10:08.649 --> 00:10:09.900
Oh, yeah, it wasn't cost that was stopping
people.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.300
Danielle: You were from a, what, middle class,
were you?

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.300
You worked in a paper...

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:15.850
Jordan: Yeah, my father was a teacher, and
my mother was a librarian, though she had

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:16.850
trained as a nurse.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:21.810
So, you know, we had a comfortable...I would
say a suburban lifestyle, essentially.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:25.539
You know, a moderate, middle class, suburban
lifestyle.

00:10:25.539 --> 00:10:29.040
That's what Fairview looked like, it looked
like a suburb that was built mostly in the,

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.529
say, between the 1950s and the 1970s.

00:10:31.529 --> 00:10:32.529
So...

00:10:32.529 --> 00:10:33.529
Danielle: Mm-hmm.

00:10:33.529 --> 00:10:38.550
But the young Jordan, and the then young Tammy,
and you have to tell us that story, how you

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:39.550
met.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:40.550
But, wanted more...

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:45.131
Jordan: Well, you know, I think that's one
thing that is different, to some degree, about

00:10:45.131 --> 00:10:46.920
class.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:53.920
My father and my mother had both left the
towns they were from, and they were forward,

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.509
future looking people.

00:10:55.509 --> 00:11:02.940
And, you know, most of my friends who quit
school and who didn't attend university, they

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:07.680
didn't have that sense, I would say, that
more developed sense of a world outside of

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:08.720
what they knew.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.850
And the other thing is that my father took
us on long trips when I was a kid.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:18.850
He was a teacher and so he had summer holidays,
and we drove all over Western Canada, and

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:20.079
down into the U.S.

00:11:20.079 --> 00:11:21.890
Long driving trips, thousands of miles.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:25.970
And, you know, that also gave us the sense
that the world was a bigger place.

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:30.880
But I knew way before I was 12, I believe,
that I was off, at least to university.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:35.950
And I think generally, in your family, if
you're liable to go to university, people

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:37.440
don't even really talk about it.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.899
It's just a given that that's what's going
to happen.

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:45.520
It's something that you take in with every
breath, almost.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:52.650
It's often an unspoken expectation, and maybe
people make casual reference like, "Well,

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:57.070
when you go to college..." but it's not like
there's a question about it.

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:02.279
Whereas if you're from a working class background,
especially if your family hasn't pursued post-secondary

00:12:02.279 --> 00:12:07.329
education, that isn't in the realm of unspoken
or spoken expectation.

00:12:07.329 --> 00:12:11.829
And it wasn't like lots of my friends, including
many of them who dropped out before they hit

00:12:11.829 --> 00:12:17.040
high school, they weren't, by no means, the
dimmest people in the class.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.540
Like, they were plenty smart.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:29.730
But they weren't oriented towards the idea
of pursuing a career that involved intellectual...what,

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:32.450
intellectual engagement wasn't in their worldview.

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:39.260
And, you know, when you hear people on the,
let's say, more socialist end of the distribution

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:43.090
talk about barriers to education, they often
talk about cost.

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:46.000
And sometimes cost is a barrier, and it's
more of a barrier...

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:47.000
Danielle: Certainly in the United States.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:50.019
Jordan: Yeah, and it's more of a barrier...although,
there's still plenty of community colleges,

00:12:50.019 --> 00:12:54.930
state colleges, where you can get educated
for a perfectly reasonable amount of money.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:58.569
But for my friends, it was never a reason.

00:12:58.569 --> 00:13:01.470
Money was never a reason they didn't pursue
post-secondary education.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:07.360
It was more like a truncated view of time,
I would say.

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:10.681
You know, there was more of an emphasis on
the here-and-now.

00:13:10.681 --> 00:13:11.850
Danielle: Well, and there were jobs aplenty,
I guess.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:13.160
Jordan: Well, there's also that, yeah.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:14.949
You know, and well-paying jobs.

00:13:14.949 --> 00:13:18.940
Like, it wasn't obvious that you were in better
shape economically to go to university than

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:19.940
you were to...

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:20.940
Danielle: Or probably worse.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:21.940
Jordan: Oh, yeah.

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:23.339
Well, especially if you were doing something
like working on the oil rigs.

00:13:23.339 --> 00:13:24.629
Danielle: Right, right.

00:13:24.629 --> 00:13:27.550
Jordan: But, you know, that was rough, cold,
harsh work.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:33.649
And it wasn't...once you had an in, you could
stay employed, but it wasn't that easy to

00:13:33.649 --> 00:13:36.000
land an entry-level job, either.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:42.819
And so...yeah, well, it was wise for lots
of working class people to work in those jobs,

00:13:42.819 --> 00:13:45.649
because they were unbelievably lucrative.

00:13:45.649 --> 00:13:49.560
And they should have been, because they were
very difficult and dangerous, and frigid cold,

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:50.560
and rough.

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:53.110
So, you know, it's not like the people didn't
earn their money.

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:56.759
Christina: Well, just tell us quickly, like,
how you met your wife.

00:13:56.759 --> 00:13:59.720
You met her when you were seven or eight,
or...a little...

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:00.720
Jordan: Yeah, in grade three.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:02.040
Christina: In grade three?

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.040
Jordan: Yeah.

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:05.960
Christina: And did you fall in love with her
in grade three?

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:06.960
Danielle: In grade three?

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:07.960
Jordan: Yeah.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:10.530
Danielle: And was it mutual?

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:11.590
Jordan: Mmm...

00:14:11.590 --> 00:14:13.209
Danielle: Not in the beginning.

00:14:13.209 --> 00:14:15.329
Jordan: She wouldn't admit it, if it was.

00:14:15.329 --> 00:14:18.509
There were lots of the boys in grade three
were in love with her.

00:14:18.509 --> 00:14:22.750
She had a whole little crew of guys that were
perfectly willing to follow her around, and

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:25.279
she was perfectly willing to exploit that.

00:14:25.279 --> 00:14:27.930
She was very good at it.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.310
Yeah, she was a very popular little girl.

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.519
Christina: It's just so wonderful that you
met as children, and...

00:14:32.519 --> 00:14:34.470
Jordan: Yeah, we were friends for a long time.

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:36.990
You know, we used to play chess together,
and croquet.

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:38.899
She was a vicious croquet player.

00:14:38.899 --> 00:14:44.990
She would...I don't know if you've ever played
croquet, but if your balls touch, then you

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:49.930
can stand on yours and whack it, and then
the other person's ball will vanish off into

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:50.930
the stratosphere.

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:53.399
And she liked to knock it all the way down
the street.

00:14:53.399 --> 00:14:54.399
Christina: Oh, that's mean.

00:14:54.399 --> 00:14:55.399
That's mean.

00:14:55.399 --> 00:14:56.399
Jordan: Yeah.

00:14:56.399 --> 00:14:57.399
And then she'd laugh, and you know...

00:14:57.399 --> 00:14:59.180
So she always had a good, vicious sense of
humor.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:05.170
It's one of the things I actually admire about
my wife, when we've had our verbal disputes,

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:09.459
which, you know, have certainly happened,
she can string together a sequence of insults

00:15:09.459 --> 00:15:13.139
that's so hair-raising that you have to laugh.

00:15:13.139 --> 00:15:14.139
It's like...

00:15:14.139 --> 00:15:16.560
Christina: Did she have brothers?

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:17.560
Jordan: She did.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:19.310
She has a brother, much older, eight years
older.

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:21.850
He's quite a peaceful person.

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:22.850
And she had two sisters...

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:26.149
Christina: I find girls with brothers seem
to, can get along with guys.

00:15:26.149 --> 00:15:32.959
Because guys, they show love and affection
by insults and jabs and jeers.

00:15:32.959 --> 00:15:35.750
And if you...and I had a brother, and I sort
of learned, okay, I could...

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:38.110
But if you don't have brothers, girls are
like, "Oh, that's so rude.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:39.110
That's so..."

00:15:39.110 --> 00:15:40.110
Jordan: Yeah.

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:41.110
Christina: So, she was...

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:43.240
Jordan: Yeah, well, she has a naturally...

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:47.249
Christina: Or maybe she came by it naturally.

00:15:47.249 --> 00:15:48.759
Jordan: ...acerbic twist...

00:15:48.759 --> 00:15:50.259
Yeah, she did.

00:15:50.259 --> 00:15:53.060
Well, and her father is quite sharp-witted,
but he was a real town character.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:54.230
He's still alive.

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:58.769
And he was a real character in the town, a
real hyper-extrovert, everybody knew him.

00:15:58.769 --> 00:16:03.740
And he had a pretty good wit on him, and she
had some of that...well, still does have some

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:04.740
of that.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:06.199
So she was a, you know...

00:16:06.199 --> 00:16:12.020
Danielle: Well, aside from her acerbic humor
and her ability to whack balls...and I just

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.230
don't wanna go further on that description.

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:17.740
Many, many things that tells us about you
that...

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:18.779
Jordan: Uh-huh.

00:16:18.779 --> 00:16:21.439
Danielle: But what else attracted...

00:16:21.439 --> 00:16:23.610
I mean, you've known her pretty much your
whole life.

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:28.540
So some of the other qualities that not just
attracted you, but enable you to sustain.

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:33.490
I mean, I think every young person in this
room will wanna know, and maybe there isn't

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:34.550
one, but what's the secret?

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:36.110
What's it like to be with someone that long?

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:37.329
How do you sustain that?

00:16:37.329 --> 00:16:42.850
Jordan: Well, I think if you're fortunate,
some of it's good fortune, you know, and I

00:16:42.850 --> 00:16:44.120
would say this is true.

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:47.851
I've watched people in their relationships,
you know, personally for a long time, but

00:16:47.851 --> 00:16:51.249
also as a professional, because I've done
a lot of clinical counseling.

00:16:51.249 --> 00:16:55.920
And I mean, there's some things that need
to be a given about the relationship, I would

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:56.920
say.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:02.250
It doesn't hurt to find the other person very
attractive, you know, and that's a mysterious

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:03.250
thing.

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:07.890
We're not exactly sure what it is that produces,
let's say, chemistry between people, although

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:10.429
chemistry is definitely part of what produces
it.

00:17:10.429 --> 00:17:13.600
There's subtle things that attract people
to one another that are way below the level

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:14.600
of consciousness.

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:23.459
So for example, women don't like the odor
of men who have Rh blood factors, who if they

00:17:23.459 --> 00:17:26.880
had children with would be likely to produce
a stillborn infant.

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:29.260
Danielle: Well, that's definitely a category
on Match.com.

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:30.260
Like...

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:31.260
Jordan: Right.

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:32.260
Right.

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:34.059
Well, it's so strange, though, because you
know, you...

00:17:34.059 --> 00:17:36.010
Danielle: How does that...how do you even
know?

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:37.410
Jordan: Well, that's a good question.

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:39.580
And, you know by odor, apparently.

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:40.580
And so there's all...

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:44.752
Danielle: What if you're wearing cologne,
and you just...

00:17:44.752 --> 00:17:46.789
Jordan: Well, that...then it would depend
on what type of cologne, I guess.

00:17:46.789 --> 00:17:47.789
But...

00:17:47.789 --> 00:17:48.789
Danielle: Rh, what was it?

00:17:48.789 --> 00:17:49.789
Jordan: Right?

00:17:49.789 --> 00:17:55.070
Smell is a very strange sense, and it's very
deeply tied to very profound emotions, including

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:56.070
memory.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:18:03.120
And so you find people attractive for reasons
that you can't always determine.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.360
And so, that was part of it.

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.630
I mean, I've always found her very attractive,
and that continues.

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:12.039
And I liked her combativeness, you know?

00:18:12.039 --> 00:18:18.650
Like, I think that there's...you want someone,
I think, in a relationship that you can spar

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:20.150
with.

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:27.120
And it's partly because you have hard problems
to solve, and if the person that you're with

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:33.320
isn't willing to put forward their opinion,
then you only have half the cognitive power

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:35.200
that you would otherwise have.

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:39.909
Now, hopefully you find someone who's interestingly
different from you.

00:18:39.909 --> 00:18:44.150
Like, not so different that you can't communicate,
and you have to be careful of that.

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:48.990
But interestingly different, and then hopefully
they have the ability and the will to express

00:18:48.990 --> 00:18:50.250
their opinion.

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:55.990
And then it's, you know, then your interest
stays heightened, and there has to be that

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:57.460
tension in a relationship.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:19:02.270
You know, people think, well, I wanna get
along perfectly with my partner.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:05.340
It's like, no, you probably don't.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:08.870
You'd just get bored, and then you'd go looking
for trouble.

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:12.340
And so you want a little bit of trouble in
the relationship, and a little bit of mystery,

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:19.080
and a little bit of combativeness, and the
ability to exchange opinions forthrightly.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:23.049
And I trust her, which is a huge element.

00:19:23.049 --> 00:19:28.650
I mean, when we finally did decide to get
together permanently, we were both in our

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:29.650
later 20s.

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:35.480
And, you know, one of the things that I had
learned by that point, and insisted to her

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:39.260
about, was that we had to tell each other
the truth.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:44.750
And she took to that wholeheartedly, you know?

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:49.039
And for better and for worse, because truths
can be harsh.

00:19:49.039 --> 00:19:53.020
Christina: Does that include like, does this
outfit make me look fat?

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:57.350
Jordan: Yeah well, the truthful answer to
that is I don't answer questions that are

00:19:57.350 --> 00:19:59.860
likely to get me in trouble.

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:00.860
Yeah.

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:01.860
So...

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:04.809
Christina: I have a son who'll answer honestly,
and it's infuriating.

00:20:04.809 --> 00:20:08.880
But then I realized, if you want the truth,
talk to Tamler, he'll tell you.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:09.890
Jordan: Well, that's the thing.

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:11.659
You know, it's useful to know.

00:20:11.659 --> 00:20:13.740
Christina: Truth is empowering.

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:20.190
Truth tellers are charismatic and, you know,
actually, both my sons are, like, brutally

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:21.190
honest.

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:27.440
Which is disconcerting, but I can see that
it's made them very formidable.

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:33.240
And because of it, people trust them, and
the friendships...and just, it gives them

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:34.240
a...

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:35.240
And you've written a lot about this.

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:38.580
Jordan: Well, you know, if I tell my wife
that she looks good in an outfit, she knows

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:39.580
that I mean it.

00:20:39.580 --> 00:20:40.580
Christina: Yeah.

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:41.690
Jordan: And so there's some utility in that.

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:44.929
Danielle: And then if you're silent and say,
"I don't answer questions..." she goes, and

00:20:44.929 --> 00:20:45.929
she...

00:20:45.929 --> 00:20:46.929
Christina: She knows it.

00:20:46.929 --> 00:20:49.419
Jordan: Well, sometimes, you know, she'll
say, you know, "Do you like this?" and I'll

00:20:49.419 --> 00:20:50.890
tell her that I don't.

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:56.390
And, you know, that doesn't necessarily make
her happy in the moment.

00:20:56.390 --> 00:21:01.010
But, if I do say I like it, she knows that
I mean it.

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:05.070
And, you know, I actually like her sense of
style a lot, so it turns out that 90% of the

00:21:05.070 --> 00:21:09.630
time it's pretty easy for me to say, "Look,
I think you look great," and mean it.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:12.990
And you know, she's a fairly harsh standard-bearer,
too.

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:19.340
Like, she's insisted that I stay in whatever
reasonable physical shape I happen to be in.

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:23.900
You know, that was something that she's very
demanding of, and I would say that it's the

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:26.780
same from my side.

00:21:26.780 --> 00:21:33.620
And we've been good at negotiating, which
is, you know, what do you want from a partner,

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:34.620
fundamentally?

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:35.620
What do you want and need?

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:40.049
I mean, the first thing is is that, well hopefully,
like I said, you're blessed with the fact

00:21:40.049 --> 00:21:41.520
that you find each other attractive.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:49.090
And I think it's very difficult for the relationship
to begin or proceed or sustain itself without

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:50.380
that.

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:52.650
But having that, then what do you want?

00:21:52.650 --> 00:21:55.860
Well, you want someone that you can trust,
you want someone that you can build a view

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:59.700
of the future with, and you want someone that
you can negotiate with.

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:04.720
And that's very hard, to negotiate with people,
because they have to tell you what they think,

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:07.520
they have to know what they want, or figure
it out.

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:11.710
They have to tell you what they want, they
have to be satisfied when they get what they

00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:14.679
want, which is also a very difficult thing
to manage.

00:22:14.679 --> 00:22:18.640
And you have to continually update that, because
your life goes through different stages, and...

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:23.309
Danielle: Well, and your attraction wanes,
as we all know at our stage of life.

00:22:23.309 --> 00:22:24.549
Not fatally, necessarily...

00:22:24.549 --> 00:22:27.029
Christina: Speak for yourself.

00:22:27.029 --> 00:22:29.210
Danielle: But no, but you will go...

00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:32.049
I mean, you will not be 25 forever.

00:22:32.049 --> 00:22:34.380
So that has to be renegotiated as well.

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:35.380
Jordan: Yeah.

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:36.620
Well, and you have to work at that too, you
know?

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:41.690
And that's something that people also don't
understand, Because they tend to think that,

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:45.980
well, that all romantic interactions should
be spontaneous.

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:50.860
It's like, well, if that's your theory, then
you might as well just give up right now,

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:52.549
if you're gonna get married.

00:22:52.549 --> 00:22:57.230
Because like, the only reason you can think
that is because you don't have enough responsibility

00:22:57.230 --> 00:23:00.030
to make romantic entanglement virtually impossible.

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:04.340
And what happens when you're married, especially
when you have little kids, is that...and you

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:08.930
both have a job, let's say, is you're so busy
that the probability that you're going to

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:14.240
find time for spontaneous mutual interaction
decreases to zero.

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:17.090
And so, if that's what you're hoping for,
then you're never gonna have it.

00:23:17.090 --> 00:23:19.909
And so what you have to do is you have to
make time for each other.

00:23:19.909 --> 00:23:25.409
And you know, if you're dating, when you're
establishing a relationship, well, you put

00:23:25.409 --> 00:23:27.450
some effort into it, you know.

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:30.600
You decide that you're going to go out for
dinner, and you dress up, to some degree.

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:34.510
And, you know, you try to present yourself
to each other in some half-ways, mutually

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:36.130
acceptable manner.

00:23:36.130 --> 00:23:39.679
And you hope that there's going to be a positive
consequence of that, that you're gonna find

00:23:39.679 --> 00:23:41.150
each other attractive.

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:46.320
But then people somehow think that once they're
married, that the same amount of effort isn't

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:48.590
necessary, and that's wrong.

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:52.440
I would say more effort is necessary on the
same front.

00:23:52.440 --> 00:23:58.070
And you have to think through, it's like,
you know, if you don't wanna be bitter about

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:02.880
the intimate element of your relationship,
how much time do you have to spend together

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:04.100
each week?

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:09.250
And my rule of thumb, sort of derived from
clinical observations, is that you need to

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:12.840
spend 90 minutes a week with your partner,
talking.

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:17.780
And that means you're telling each other about
your life, and staying in touch, you know,

00:24:17.780 --> 00:24:20.659
so that you each know what the other is up
to.

00:24:20.659 --> 00:24:24.490
And you're discussing what needs to be done
to keep the household running smoothly, and

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:29.120
you're laying out some mutually acceptable
vision of how the next week or the next month's

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:30.690
are going to go together, right?

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:35.270
So that keeps your narratives locked together
like the strands in a rope.

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:38.290
You need that for 90 minutes, or you drift
apart.

00:24:38.290 --> 00:24:42.770
And you need to spend intimate time together
at least once a week, and probably more like

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:43.860
twice.

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:45.340
And that has to be negotiated.

00:24:45.340 --> 00:24:49.700
And if you don't negotiate it, and if you
don't make it a priority, then it won't happen,

00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:50.850
in all likelihood.

00:24:50.850 --> 00:24:53.659
And then, well, then you don't have it.

00:24:53.659 --> 00:24:58.300
And that's a catastrophe, because there's
not that many things in life that are, you

00:24:58.300 --> 00:25:04.090
know, intrinsically, what would you say, engaging
and meaningful and pleasurable, and also bonding,

00:25:04.090 --> 00:25:05.309
all of that.

00:25:05.309 --> 00:25:10.539
And if you let that go, then, well, part of
you dies, and part of the relationship dies.

00:25:10.539 --> 00:25:15.730
And well, then there's always the possibility
of becoming attracted by alternative entanglements,

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:19.120
which you would do if you had any spirit left,
right?

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:25.360
I mean, that's the thing is if, well, if you're
not, if your relationship at home is entirely

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:28.710
unsatisfying sexually, what are you supposed
to do with that?

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:29.710
Nothing?

00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:31.159
You're supposed to just bear it?

00:25:31.159 --> 00:25:34.360
I mean, in one way the answer is yes, because
it's your marriage.

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:38.580
But another way is, well, what, that's all
the fight you've got in you?

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:43.860
You're gonna just let the erotic element of
your life die, and accept everything that

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:49.799
goes along with that, because you're not willing
to cause a bit of trouble to ensure that it's

00:25:49.799 --> 00:25:51.440
maintained?

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:54.399
And you know, and we're not very good at thinking
these things through consciously.

00:25:54.399 --> 00:25:59.559
I mean, people are bad at negotiating, period,
as far as I can tell, but they're particularly

00:25:59.559 --> 00:26:02.680
bad at negotiating things that are deeply
private.

00:26:02.680 --> 00:26:05.510
How much do you want your partner to know
about you, anyways?

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:12.320
It takes a lot of trust to have a real conversation
about what you need and want.

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:15.440
Christina: Now, you have...

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:22.880
In the press, people read that you have a
following of young men.

00:26:22.880 --> 00:26:27.580
And I went to hear your lecture in Washington,
D.C., and there were a lot of women there.

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:33.501
And your book...and first of all, men don't
buy books that often, compared to women, so

00:26:33.501 --> 00:26:35.570
I'm presuming you have a lot of female readers.

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:40.150
And I found it, Danielle and I found it completely
readable and...

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:42.680
Jordan: Well, it wasn't written for men.

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:44.260
Christina: So, you have this...

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:47.950
Jordan: It's more like a delusional desire
on the part of the radical leftists that the

00:26:47.950 --> 00:26:51.289
only people that could possibly be attracted
to me are angry men.

00:26:51.289 --> 00:26:52.289
Christina: Exactly.

00:26:52.289 --> 00:26:54.620
Jordan: It'd be better if they were angry,
young, white men, you know?

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:56.490
Because then that fits the narrative completely,
but...

00:26:56.490 --> 00:27:00.470
Christina: But yet, you have a diverse audience,
a diverse following, including many women.

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:01.470
And...

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:02.529
Jordan: They're also not particularly angry.

00:27:02.529 --> 00:27:04.289
I mean, I've been...I've talked to 200 and...

00:27:04.289 --> 00:27:05.289
Danielle: I know.

00:27:05.289 --> 00:27:06.580
Well, you're diffusing the anger.

00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:10.100
That's the point of your book, is stop being
angry, stop being resentful, right?

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:12.960
Jordan: Well, resentment, that's absolutely
crippling.

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:17.750
Like, resentment...just, resentment, deceit,
arrogance, that's part of...I'm writing another

00:27:17.750 --> 00:27:21.500
book, and one of the rules is don't allow
yourself to become, resentful...

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:22.690
Christina: What is it again?

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:23.690
Resentful...

00:27:23.690 --> 00:27:24.690
Jordan: Resentful...

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:25.690
Christina: ...deceitful...

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:26.690
Jordan: ...deceitful, and arrogant.

00:27:26.690 --> 00:27:27.690
Yeah, those three thigns together.

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:29.990
Danielle: Yeah, but it could be rad if you
just...

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:32.870
Jordan: Well, that's supposed to be a good
thing.

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:37.330
So yeah, and I mean, there's been 250,000
people, as I said, come to the lectures, and

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:39.470
there hasn't been a single negative incident.

00:27:39.470 --> 00:27:40.470
Not one.

00:27:40.470 --> 00:27:45.409
Christina: This is what I find fascinating,
is that I found you early on...

00:27:45.409 --> 00:27:51.130
I had no idea who you were, I just thought
it was like, "Butch Cassidy and the Sundance

00:27:51.130 --> 00:27:52.130
Kid."

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:53.130
Like, who is that guy?

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:54.130
Who is that guy?

00:27:54.130 --> 00:27:56.019
Like, you were pretty good.

00:27:56.019 --> 00:28:00.490
And we were covering a lot of the same topics
later on, and wow.

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:03.789
And then, you know, I found out who you were.

00:28:03.789 --> 00:28:11.440
But it's astonishing to me, is that there's
this amazing, just, split between the positivity

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:17.560
of your audience, the diversity of your audience,
the intellectual content of your message,

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:22.370
and then you get with a snarky journalist
with an agenda, and I'm not mentioning names,

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:23.370
but this...

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:24.470
Danielle: Phoebe C...

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:25.730
Christina: No, Phoebe...

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:29.110
No, this young woman from GQ...

00:28:29.110 --> 00:28:30.110
Jordan: Oh, yeah.

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:31.110
Christina: ...and it was just...

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:32.110
Jordan: She hated me on sight.

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:33.110
Christina: On sight.

00:28:33.110 --> 00:28:34.142
And it was just like gotcha, gotcha, gotcha.

00:28:34.142 --> 00:28:35.142
Danielle: So did the BBC lady.

00:28:35.142 --> 00:28:36.142
Jordan: Yeah, that was Channel 4.

00:28:36.142 --> 00:28:37.409
We don't wanna blame the BBC.

00:28:37.409 --> 00:28:38.409
Danielle: True.

00:28:38.409 --> 00:28:39.409
Jordan: It was Channel 4.

00:28:39.409 --> 00:28:40.549
Christina: You are a saint.

00:28:40.549 --> 00:28:41.549
And then I...

00:28:41.549 --> 00:28:47.269
And she seemed, you know, as often, she seemed
intelligent and capable of insight, up to

00:28:47.269 --> 00:28:48.269
a point.

00:28:48.269 --> 00:28:51.840
But it's almost as if something had seized
her mind, and she...

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:52.840
Jordan: Oh, yeah.

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:53.840
Something had.

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:54.840
Christina: Something had.

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:55.840
Jordan: That's for sure.

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:56.840
You bet.

00:28:56.840 --> 00:28:57.840
Christina: Camille Paglia...

00:28:57.840 --> 00:28:58.840
Jordan: Possessed by ideology.

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:01.700
Christina: Camille Paglia says that a whole
generation, some of our most talented young

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:05.120
women are incapable of thought, because of
this ideology.

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:06.919
Danielle: Well, different thought, maybe you
mean to say.

00:29:06.919 --> 00:29:09.910
Christina: Or just an openness to...

00:29:09.910 --> 00:29:10.910
She couldn't...

00:29:10.910 --> 00:29:15.289
And you were saying completely, like, interesting,
fascinating, original things.

00:29:15.289 --> 00:29:18.980
Even to me, who've studied these topics.

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:19.980
And I thought wow, and...

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:20.980
Jordan: No, it was quite the day.

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:21.980
So I went to Baltimore, and...

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:22.980
Christina: You survived it very well.

00:29:22.980 --> 00:29:23.980
You acquitted yourself beautifully.

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:25.950
Jordan: Yeah well, it made me think a lot
that day.

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:29.650
Because I went there, and I had to go out
of my way to do it.

00:29:29.650 --> 00:29:32.840
Not that I'm complaining, but there's a reason
for saying that.

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:33.840
You know, so I go out there...

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:34.890
Christina: You had to go to Baltimore?

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:35.890
Jordan: Yeah.

00:29:35.890 --> 00:29:36.890
Well, I was talking in Baltimore.

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:37.890
Christina: Oh, okay.

00:29:37.890 --> 00:29:38.890
Danielle: There's the aquarium.

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:42.789
Jordan: And I showed up to the hotel room
where this was all occurring.

00:29:42.789 --> 00:29:47.751
And you know, what you expect, generally speaking,
even from journalists who aren't...you know,

00:29:47.751 --> 00:29:54.110
who are more of the attack dog variety, or
who maybe aren't positively predisposed to

00:29:54.110 --> 00:30:00.610
you ideologically or personally, you expect
a certain modicum of professional politeness.

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:06.059
You know, because, well, you don't have to
be there, and you came, and you accepted an

00:30:06.059 --> 00:30:07.309
invitation, and all of that.

00:30:07.309 --> 00:30:15.250
And so, even with the Channel 4 journalist,
Cathy Newman, she was quite polite and forthcoming

00:30:15.250 --> 00:30:18.559
in the green room, before the interview, you
know.

00:30:18.559 --> 00:30:23.350
So she had at least that professional persona,
which is...it's not nothing, right?

00:30:23.350 --> 00:30:29.400
There's something to be said for going through
the motions professionally, in an appropriate

00:30:29.400 --> 00:30:30.400
manner.

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:36.460
But when I walked into the hotel room in Baltimore,
it was obvious that this interviewer had already

00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:42.220
made up her mind about me a hundred percent,
and that she was absolutely, you know, negatively

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:44.621
predisposed to me, with a personal animus.

00:30:44.621 --> 00:30:46.720
An animus is exactly the right word.

00:30:46.720 --> 00:30:52.750
And there was about a half an hour photography
session, because it was GQ, and so I was in

00:30:52.750 --> 00:30:53.750
that atmosphere...

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:55.110
The photographers were fine.

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:59.140
I was in that atmosphere for about 45 minutes
before we started to talk.

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:06.740
And part of the reason that I'm so...I'm not
as calm during that interview as I usually

00:31:06.740 --> 00:31:08.130
am, I'm a little bit harsher.

00:31:08.130 --> 00:31:14.130
And the reason for that is that, you know,
it just started off instantly combative.

00:31:14.130 --> 00:31:16.440
And what I should have done...

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:23.620
You see, it's very, very difficult to be awake
enough to do these things properly.

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:27.320
And the interview progressed fine, although
by the end of it I'd thought that I had maybe

00:31:27.320 --> 00:31:32.000
done enough interviews for a while, because
I didn't think I had regulated my temper as

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:34.090
well during that interview as I might have.

00:31:34.090 --> 00:31:35.090
I was harsher.

00:31:35.090 --> 00:31:36.090
Christina: You actually did.

00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:37.090
Jordan: Well, it's not so bad...

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:39.019
Christina: For the first few minutes you were
getting angry, and then she brought up a question

00:31:39.019 --> 00:31:40.899
about anger.

00:31:40.899 --> 00:31:44.899
And I just saw you kind of adjust, and then
after that it was smooth sailing.

00:31:44.899 --> 00:31:47.380
Jordan: Well, that's good, because it was
touch and go, you know?

00:31:47.380 --> 00:31:49.850
And I thought, boy, you know, maybe you're
running out of patience.

00:31:49.850 --> 00:31:53.149
Maybe you, you know, maybe it's time to dial
back on the interviews.

00:31:53.149 --> 00:31:57.230
Because, you know, I've had many interviews
like that, and they're very...I find them...

00:31:57.230 --> 00:32:00.464
Like, and it takes me, like, three days to
recover from an interview like that.

00:32:00.464 --> 00:32:01.464
Christina: I know.

00:32:01.464 --> 00:32:04.389
And then you start thinking to yourself, like,
what I should have said, I should have said

00:32:04.389 --> 00:32:05.389
that.

00:32:05.389 --> 00:32:06.389
And I drive myself mad.

00:32:06.389 --> 00:32:07.389
No, but you did very well.

00:32:07.389 --> 00:32:13.049
But it's so interesting that...what it told
me was how parochial she was, and she lives

00:32:13.049 --> 00:32:14.080
in her own little world.

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:18.259
Danielle: Well, Christina, isn't it more a
little bit about the ideology of our time?

00:32:18.259 --> 00:32:23.210
And gosh, you encounter this everywhere, and
I used to write about this, wisely, I would

00:32:23.210 --> 00:32:24.210
I encounter it.

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:31.840
I mean, I think part of the issue is that
you will acknowledge that there are differences

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:33.130
between the sexes.

00:32:33.130 --> 00:32:34.130
That seems to be...

00:32:34.130 --> 00:32:35.960
Jordan: No, that's a hell of a sexist thing
to do, really.

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:36.960
Danielle: ...the heresy.

00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:37.960
No, that's a heresy.

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:47.200
Because when I was reading your book, there's
nothing about it that is anti-female...

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:51.799
In fact, you do a lot of examination of the
Adam and Eve story, and you have this wonderful

00:32:51.799 --> 00:32:58.220
passage about, like, Adam, being the originally
aggrieved man who throws the woman under the

00:32:58.220 --> 00:32:59.220
bus, you know?

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:00.220
And...

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:01.220
Jordan: Yeah, yeah, no.

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:02.220
Immediately.

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:03.220
It's her fault.

00:33:03.220 --> 00:33:04.220
Danielle: Yeah, it's her fault.

00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:05.220
Jordan: And God, you too.

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:06.220
You made her, it's your fault, too, that I'm
hiding.

00:33:06.220 --> 00:33:07.220
Yeah, it's really funny.

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:09.230
Danielle: So there's nothing in this.

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:13.980
And the rules, such as they are, you know,
they seem very common sensical, they could

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:15.510
apply to anyone.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:21.539
So is that a fair surmise of why you get so
attacked?

00:33:21.539 --> 00:33:27.940
That just the very fact that you're willing
to speak about the sexes as being not unequal

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:28.940
but different...

00:33:28.940 --> 00:33:29.940
Christina: Different but equal.

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:30.940
Danielle: Yeah.

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:37.059
Jordan: Well, you know, I would say that that's
part of it, because there's a threat there.

00:33:37.059 --> 00:33:42.940
So, one of the things that happened when I
was in Scandinavia, I just wrote a column

00:33:42.940 --> 00:33:43.940
about this, actually.

00:33:43.940 --> 00:33:47.990
It was interesting being in Scandinavia, especially
in Sweden, because they've pushed the equality

00:33:47.990 --> 00:33:51.970
of opportunity doctrine farther than any other
country in the world.

00:33:51.970 --> 00:33:52.970
Christina: They invented it.

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:58.259
It almost started there, like, in the U.N.,
in the original, you know, charter, the Swedes

00:33:58.259 --> 00:33:59.259
were there.

00:33:59.259 --> 00:34:00.669
And they've never given up.

00:34:00.669 --> 00:34:01.809
Jordan: No, no.

00:34:01.809 --> 00:34:07.690
And so, the week that I was there was the
same week that two articles were published

00:34:07.690 --> 00:34:12.020
on gender differences in temperament, and
in interest.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:20.480
And the biggest sex differences that we know
of that aren't morphological are in interest.

00:34:20.480 --> 00:34:24.869
So women are more interested in people, by
and large, and men are more interested in

00:34:24.869 --> 00:34:26.600
things, by and large.

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:29.780
And the difference is actually large, it's
one standard deviation.

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:35.200
And so that means if you're a man, you would
have to be more interested in people than

00:34:35.200 --> 00:34:40.490
85% of men to be as interested as 50% of women.

00:34:40.490 --> 00:34:45.589
And if you're a woman, you'd have to be more
interested in things than 85% of women to

00:34:45.589 --> 00:34:48.829
be as interested as the 50th percentile male.

00:34:48.829 --> 00:34:54.349
So the difference is actually quite substantial,
and it's certainly large enough to drive occupational

00:34:54.349 --> 00:34:55.990
choice differences, which it does.

00:34:55.990 --> 00:35:00.510
Christina: And it explains a lot about the
configuration of people in the workplace.

00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:01.510
Jordan: Oh, absolutely.

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:07.369
Well, and you know, we're approaching parody,
in terms of overall workplace distribution

00:35:07.369 --> 00:35:08.369
of men and women.

00:35:08.369 --> 00:35:10.690
But there's massive differences in occupational
choice.

00:35:10.690 --> 00:35:16.490
Like, it's very interesting, for example,
to go to the website of the U.S. Labor Department

00:35:16.490 --> 00:35:19.569
and look at male and female dominated industries.

00:35:19.569 --> 00:35:25.460
And you know, the top 10 male dominated industries
have basically zero women in them.

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:28.119
So, bricklayers being one of them.

00:35:28.119 --> 00:35:31.740
Christina: People...like, people, they're
people-free zones, according to Camille Paglia,

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:34.690
that you find just a lot of men in the people-free
zones.

00:35:34.690 --> 00:35:35.690
Jordan: Yeah, yeah.

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:36.690
Yeah.

00:35:36.690 --> 00:35:37.690
Yeah I...

00:35:37.690 --> 00:35:39.560
Christina: And the women are...and you ask
a group of women and men, "Would you rather

00:35:39.560 --> 00:35:44.300
spend the next three weeks taking apart a
machine and putting it together, or helping

00:35:44.300 --> 00:35:46.540
a group of people work out their problems?"

00:35:46.540 --> 00:35:51.330
And the pool of people who wanna do the ma-...it's
just far more men than women...

00:35:51.330 --> 00:35:55.670
Jordan: Well, and there's more men in women-dominated
industries than there are women in men-dominated

00:35:55.670 --> 00:35:56.670
industries, at the extreme.

00:35:56.670 --> 00:35:57.670
So that's kind of interesting...

00:35:57.670 --> 00:35:59.099
Danielle: So you mean, like, would that be
like nursing?

00:35:59.099 --> 00:36:00.099
Jordan: Nursing, yeah.

00:36:00.099 --> 00:36:01.954
So, there's way more male nurses than there
are female bricklayers.

00:36:01.954 --> 00:36:05.329
Christina: But the male nurses...I've studied
these male nurses.

00:36:05.329 --> 00:36:11.630
And already, you know, gender activists are
upset because they earn more than women.

00:36:11.630 --> 00:36:12.630
Jordan: Mm-hmm.

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:17.900
Christina: And a professor of nursing at University
of Pennsylvania tried to find out why, and

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:21.880
she found out they immediately find out what's
the best paying field, sub-field.

00:36:21.880 --> 00:36:22.954
So they go into, like, nurse anesthesiology.

00:36:22.954 --> 00:36:24.410
It pays a lot more.

00:36:24.410 --> 00:36:28.040
The men are there in disproportionate number.

00:36:28.040 --> 00:36:31.829
And they're willing to work, you know, insane
hours, they're far more willing to move to

00:36:31.829 --> 00:36:32.829
a higher paying...

00:36:32.829 --> 00:36:33.829
Jordan: To move.

00:36:33.829 --> 00:36:36.550
Yeah, it's the same thing Farrell with gender
differences, is that men are more willing

00:36:36.550 --> 00:36:39.720
to move, they're more willing to work longer
hours...

00:36:39.720 --> 00:36:44.420
Yep, they're more willing to work outside,
they're more willing to take on dangerous

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:48.010
tasks, they're more likely to work in scalable
industries.

00:36:48.010 --> 00:36:52.050
So like, you can't scale personal care, it's
very, very difficult.

00:36:52.050 --> 00:36:53.920
They're much less likely to work part-time.

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:57.580
If they have small businesses, they're much
more likely to work full-time in the small

00:36:57.580 --> 00:37:00.280
business, rather than part-time.

00:37:00.280 --> 00:37:03.359
And I mean, women have their reasons to wanna
work part time.

00:37:03.359 --> 00:37:09.000
And Farrell also pointed out that if you work
10% longer hours, you make 40% more money,

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:11.380
in non-linear return on overtime.

00:37:11.380 --> 00:37:13.350
That's something that's really useful to know,
you know, in terms of your career planning.

00:37:13.350 --> 00:37:14.599
Christina: It's hugely beneficial to an employer.

00:37:14.599 --> 00:37:15.890
That, have someone...

00:37:15.890 --> 00:37:18.740
Jordan: Well, it also marks you out, you know?

00:37:18.740 --> 00:37:22.460
Like, if you have 10 employees, and they're
all doing a reasonable job, let's say, but

00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:26.359
one of them is working an extra half an hour
a day, or 45 minutes a day, and you can observe

00:37:26.359 --> 00:37:31.490
that every day, then that gives them an edge
with regards to potential promotion.

00:37:31.490 --> 00:37:34.470
And the return on those edges is non-linear.

00:37:34.470 --> 00:37:39.190
And so, anyway, so I went to Scandinavia,
and it was the same week that two studies

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:45.890
were released, showing what had already been
established beyond a shadow of a doubt, that

00:37:45.890 --> 00:37:50.010
the personality differences between men and
women, and the differences in interest as

00:37:50.010 --> 00:37:56.740
well actually get bigger as your society gets
richer, and as it gets more egalitarian.

00:37:56.740 --> 00:37:58.310
And not just a little bit, either.

00:37:58.310 --> 00:38:02.500
That's the other thing that's so interesting,
is you might think, well, the effect is...it's

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:05.829
the opposite of what the social constructionists
would predict, first of all.

00:38:05.829 --> 00:38:10.109
So that's the first thing to point out, is
it's not only that their hypothesis wasn't

00:38:10.109 --> 00:38:13.859
supported, it was decidedly refuted.

00:38:13.859 --> 00:38:16.550
And none of them have come to terms with that.

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:18.180
And it's not a small effect.

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:23.680
The difference between personality, between
men and women in Scandinavia, is a lot larger

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:25.410
than it is in non-egalitarian countries.

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:26.410
Christina: Like in rural Botswana.

00:38:26.410 --> 00:38:27.410
Jordan: Mm-hmm, yeah.

00:38:27.410 --> 00:38:29.280
Danielle: But that's also true in the United
States.

00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:36.180
The richer the demograph of your household,
the more likely the woman is to take time

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:39.150
out, and be at home with the kids, because
she can afford to do it.

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:40.150
Jordan: She can afford it, yeah.

00:38:40.150 --> 00:38:43.520
Christina: She can afford to do it, and she
can afford to major in odd, you know, low-paying

00:38:43.520 --> 00:38:47.240
fields, like, I don't know, feminist dance
therapy, or something.

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:52.000
Jordan: Well, the other thing you see, too,
is that...one of the things that's also interesting,

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:57.079
I think, is that, you know, there's this idea
that marriage is a patriarchal institution,

00:38:57.079 --> 00:38:58.079
you know?

00:38:58.079 --> 00:39:00.990
That's primarily put there for the utility
of the male.

00:39:00.990 --> 00:39:05.650
And like, I think that's complete, bloody
rubbish, and I don't think there's any evidence

00:39:05.650 --> 00:39:06.720
to support it at all.

00:39:06.720 --> 00:39:11.400
But I think the best counter-evidence is that,
well, if that's the case, then rich people

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:16.079
shouldn't be getting married, because they
don't have to oppress themselves.

00:39:16.079 --> 00:39:20.190
But the truth of the matter is is that the
higher your demographic position, the more

00:39:20.190 --> 00:39:21.870
likely you are to be married.

00:39:21.870 --> 00:39:23.720
So marriage has falling apart among, you know...

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:26.220
Christina: And the more likely the wife is
staying home.

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:30.080
But not...I mean, she has all sorts of pursuits,
but she's not...

00:39:30.080 --> 00:39:34.810
Danielle: Well, there's an old saying, "Any
woman who marries for money, earns it."

00:39:34.810 --> 00:39:35.810
Jordan: Yeah.

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:37.020
Danielle: But now, let me just...

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:39.310
Okay, this is where you might get in a little
trouble.

00:39:39.310 --> 00:39:43.329
Because in your book, you call men order and
women chaos.

00:39:43.329 --> 00:39:49.319
And you say, "Order, the known, appears symbolically
associated with masculinity, and chaos, the

00:39:49.319 --> 00:39:52.569
eternal feminine, is also the crushing force
of sexual selection."

00:39:52.569 --> 00:39:53.660
Christina: Yeah, what's up with that?

00:39:53.660 --> 00:39:54.660
Danielle: Yeah?

00:39:54.660 --> 00:39:55.660
Christina: Are we chaotic?

00:39:55.660 --> 00:39:56.660
Danielle: Do you find us chaotic?

00:39:56.660 --> 00:40:00.819
Jordan: Well, it isn't men and women that
are order and chaos, it's masculinity and

00:40:00.819 --> 00:40:02.430
femininity, symbolically.

00:40:02.430 --> 00:40:11.740
And so what's happened, fundamentally, is
that our brains are wired for social cognition.

00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:14.730
So we're not natural scientists, we're natural
sociologists.

00:40:14.730 --> 00:40:19.140
That might be a better...even though I shudder
to think that that might be true.

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:21.670
Christina: Especially given the state of sociology.

00:40:21.670 --> 00:40:22.670
Jordan: Maybe...

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:23.670
Yes, well, that's...yeah.

00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:24.670
Hello.

00:40:24.670 --> 00:40:25.670
Christina: Okay, triggering.

00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:26.670
Triggering.

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:27.670
Jordan: Yeah.

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:31.670
Or maybe we're more naturally people who observe
through the lens of fiction, and that what

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:34.619
we see as the world, as characterized.

00:40:34.619 --> 00:40:40.300
And the world, obviously, is made out of men
and women and children, and those seem to

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:48.170
be our fundamental cognitive categories, masculinity,
femininity, and then the category of children.

00:40:48.170 --> 00:40:57.890
And those categories have expanded to take
on connotations outside of pure person perception.

00:40:57.890 --> 00:41:02.589
And so, you know, it's for this reason that
if you go to a movie, and maybe it's a Disney

00:41:02.589 --> 00:41:07.770
animated movie, and I like to talk about those
because they draw in a very deep, symbolic

00:41:07.770 --> 00:41:13.250
well, it's perfectly reasonable to see a witch
that lives in a swamp, because those go together.

00:41:13.250 --> 00:41:15.540
Like, it makes sense, you know?

00:41:15.540 --> 00:41:17.730
The witch doesn't live in a gleaming chrome
high-rise.

00:41:17.730 --> 00:41:21.050
You know, she lives in a swamp, because that's...and
maybe in a shack...

00:41:21.050 --> 00:41:22.050
Danielle: Oh, it would be handy for her broom,
I think.

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:23.480
She could just fly out the door.

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:24.480
Jordan: Well, that's it...that's...

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:29.020
Well, the high-rise would be better for the
broom, right, because you could take off better.

00:41:29.020 --> 00:41:34.829
But there are categories of symbolic association
that are natural to the way we think.

00:41:34.829 --> 00:41:39.140
And the fundamental elements of those categories
seem to be gendered.

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:42.520
And so this is partly why I make reference
to Taoism, for example.

00:41:42.520 --> 00:41:46.319
So for the Taoists, the world is made out
of chaos and order.

00:41:46.319 --> 00:41:51.619
And chaos is the domain that you don't understand
and that emerges unpredictably, but also the

00:41:51.619 --> 00:41:58.280
domain from which new forms emerge, right,
because it's from novelty that the new emerges.

00:41:58.280 --> 00:42:03.730
And I think the fundamental association between
femininity as chaos is the association between

00:42:03.730 --> 00:42:06.849
what's unexpected and novel, and what's new.

00:42:06.849 --> 00:42:09.579
Because new forms emerge from chaos.

00:42:09.579 --> 00:42:14.349
And it's not that chaos is bad and order is
good.

00:42:14.349 --> 00:42:15.349
That's not...

00:42:15.349 --> 00:42:16.900
Christina: No, both have their pathologies,
and their virtues.

00:42:16.900 --> 00:42:17.900
Yes, of course.

00:42:17.900 --> 00:42:19.220
Jordan: Both have their pathologies.

00:42:19.220 --> 00:42:24.110
Yes, and what you're looking for, and this
is what the book concentrates on above all,

00:42:24.110 --> 00:42:27.540
is that you're looking constantly to find
the balance between those two.

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:34.280
So for example, formally speaking, the domain
of order is that place that you are when what

00:42:34.280 --> 00:42:38.290
you're doing is producing the results that
you want to have produced.

00:42:38.290 --> 00:42:42.760
So imagine...think about the preconditions
for not being anxious.

00:42:42.760 --> 00:42:46.990
Okay, so the preconditions are that you're
constantly making predictions about what's

00:42:46.990 --> 00:42:52.839
going to happen next, and those predictions
are tied tightly to your behavioral output.

00:42:52.839 --> 00:42:57.330
So you act in a certain way, and you presume
that a certain thing is going to happen.

00:42:57.330 --> 00:43:02.480
And if your actions produce the results that
you desire, then you assume that you know,

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:05.940
where you are, and you know, what you're doing,
and that your plan is intact, and that the

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:07.280
environment is secure.

00:43:07.280 --> 00:43:09.780
And that keeps your anxiety under control.

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:11.500
That's order.

00:43:11.500 --> 00:43:15.490
And then, you know, maybe you're at a party
and you don't know anybody, and you tell a

00:43:15.490 --> 00:43:20.309
joke, and everybody looks at you, like...what
you said was not only not funny, but also

00:43:20.309 --> 00:43:21.839
downright offensive.

00:43:21.839 --> 00:43:26.119
And then all of a sudden, you've moved from
the domain of order into the domain of chaos.

00:43:26.119 --> 00:43:30.260
Because you thought you were somewhere, and
you thought you were someone, and you thought

00:43:30.260 --> 00:43:35.570
you were with people that were of a certain
type, and you got all that wrong.

00:43:35.570 --> 00:43:36.570
And so it calls...

00:43:36.570 --> 00:43:39.340
Danielle: Well, you're also suggesting it
is gonna be the woman who says, "I find that

00:43:39.340 --> 00:43:40.560
really offensive."

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:42.672
Jordan: I'm not suggesting that, but...

00:43:42.672 --> 00:43:43.940
Christina: But you know, it probably is.

00:43:43.940 --> 00:43:45.190
Danielle: It probably was, yeah.

00:43:45.190 --> 00:43:46.190
Christina: Never mind.

00:43:46.190 --> 00:43:50.600
Jordan: But women are also more sensitive
to negative emotion, so there is some slightly

00:43:50.600 --> 00:43:52.950
higher probability that that might be the
case.

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:58.089
But then I think women are also associated,
at least in men's imaginations, with nature,

00:43:58.089 --> 00:44:03.540
which is part of the chaotic domain, say,
as opposed to culture, because they're sexually

00:44:03.540 --> 00:44:04.540
selective.

00:44:04.540 --> 00:44:07.280
So you gotta think, what is nature?

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:10.290
I mean, we have that as a cognitive category,
right?

00:44:10.290 --> 00:44:13.950
We think of the natural world, and we think
of nature vs. culture, it's a fundamental

00:44:13.950 --> 00:44:15.140
opposition.

00:44:15.140 --> 00:44:16.140
What is nature?

00:44:16.140 --> 00:44:20.130
Well, nature is trees and landscapes and animals,
and all of that.

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:22.880
But that isn't what nature fundamentally is.

00:44:22.880 --> 00:44:28.840
Nature, fundamentally, is that which selects
from a genetic perspective.

00:44:28.840 --> 00:44:31.880
That's nature, that's the fundamental definition
of nature.

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:36.440
And it is the case that human females are
sexually selective.

00:44:36.440 --> 00:44:39.079
And it's a major component of human behavior.

00:44:39.079 --> 00:44:48.420
So the evolutionary theory, roughly speaking,
is that the reason we diverged from chimpanzees

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:54.740
eight million years ago, seven million years
ago, is at least in part because of the differences

00:44:54.740 --> 00:45:01.040
between sexual selectivity between female
humans and female chimpanzees.

00:45:01.040 --> 00:45:06.300
Female chimpanzees are more likely to have
offspring from dominant males, but it's not

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:08.360
because of their sexual selectively.

00:45:08.360 --> 00:45:14.609
So a female chimpanzee has periods of fertility
that are marked by physical, by observable

00:45:14.609 --> 00:45:16.089
physiological changes.

00:45:16.089 --> 00:45:18.250
Not the case with human females.

00:45:18.250 --> 00:45:24.550
Human female ovulation is concealed, so that's
a very profound biological difference between

00:45:24.550 --> 00:45:26.599
human females and chimpanzees.

00:45:26.599 --> 00:45:30.609
And the chimpanzee females will mate with
any male, but the dominant males chase the

00:45:30.609 --> 00:45:32.900
subordinate males away.

00:45:32.900 --> 00:45:35.530
But human females are sexually selective.

00:45:35.530 --> 00:45:37.180
And it's not a trivial fact.

00:45:37.180 --> 00:45:42.910
So you have twice as many female ancestors
as male ancestors.

00:45:42.910 --> 00:45:44.940
You think, well, how can that be?

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:49.440
Well, imagine that on average, every single
human female has had one child throughout

00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:53.550
the entire course of history, which is approximately
correct, by the way.

00:45:53.550 --> 00:45:58.089
Then imagine that half of the men had zero,
and the other half had two.

00:45:58.089 --> 00:46:01.460
Okay, and that's roughly the case.

00:46:01.460 --> 00:46:06.180
So, half of males, historically speaking,
have been reproductive disasters.

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:09.630
And the reason for that is because of female
sexual selectivity.

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:13.100
So it is actually the case that female humans
are nature.

00:46:13.100 --> 00:46:17.230
It's not only that they're associated with
nature symbolically.

00:46:17.230 --> 00:46:22.990
As far as production is concerned, they are
the force of nature that does the selection.

00:46:22.990 --> 00:46:25.700
And so they're nature, in the most fundamental
way.

00:46:25.700 --> 00:46:28.750
And there is a chaotic element of that, at
least in relationship to men.

00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:35.390
And also in relationship to women, because
a lot of the female-on-female competition

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:40.670
is competition that's chaotic for the right
to be sexually selective, right?

00:46:40.670 --> 00:46:45.490
Not only with regards to men, which drives
a lot of politicking, but also in relationship

00:46:45.490 --> 00:46:46.490
to each other.

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:51.630
Because part of what human females do is jockey
for position in the female dominance hierarchy

00:46:51.630 --> 00:46:57.310
for the top position, which is the woman who
gets to be most sexually selective.

00:46:57.310 --> 00:47:01.320
And so that drives female-female competition,
and it's a different dynamic.

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:05.890
There's similarities between female-female
competition and male-male competition, but

00:47:05.890 --> 00:47:08.030
there're also differences, and they're pronounced.

00:47:08.030 --> 00:47:14.280
So men, for example, well, men are more likely
to compete for socio-economic status, and

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:16.950
that's partly because that drives female mate
choice.

00:47:16.950 --> 00:47:22.809
So the correlation for men between socio-economic
status and sexual success is about .6.

00:47:22.809 --> 00:47:23.950
And for women, it's zero.

00:47:23.950 --> 00:47:24.950
Zero.

00:47:24.950 --> 00:47:27.810
In fact, it's actually slightly negative.

00:47:27.810 --> 00:47:30.049
And that's a huge difference between men and
women.

00:47:30.049 --> 00:47:33.079
Christina: Do you know the anthropologist
Sarah Hrdy?

00:47:33.079 --> 00:47:34.079
H-R-D-Y.

00:47:34.079 --> 00:47:39.549
And she's, like, my favorite feminist theorist,
although as she would say, "I'm a theorist

00:47:39.549 --> 00:47:40.559
who happens to be a feminist."

00:47:40.559 --> 00:47:47.100
But she studied primate behavior, and she
watched, she looked at the women very care-...

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:54.380
the females, not women, very carefully, and
looked at chimpanzees and gazelles, and stuff

00:47:54.380 --> 00:47:55.380
like that.

00:47:55.380 --> 00:47:57.910
And found that the female...

00:47:57.910 --> 00:48:05.119
Initially, like, male primatologists would
look and say, oh, the males are dominant and

00:48:05.119 --> 00:48:06.690
females are so cooperative.

00:48:06.690 --> 00:48:10.210
She looked more carefully, and saw the females
weren't exactly cooperative.

00:48:10.210 --> 00:48:17.740
Like, they would pass around their infants,
their baby, you know, whatever they were,

00:48:17.740 --> 00:48:18.740
and would find...

00:48:18.740 --> 00:48:23.460
And so the male primatologists would say,
oh they're so kind and caring.

00:48:23.460 --> 00:48:29.400
She found out that when it was not hers, they
would take, like, little tufts of hair that

00:48:29.400 --> 00:48:34.049
would come, or they'd do something to the
eyes, and the baby would, like, be injured.

00:48:34.049 --> 00:48:35.880
And she saw all this violence that was covert...

00:48:35.880 --> 00:48:37.360
Jordan: Especially true when there's status
differentiation.

00:48:37.360 --> 00:48:38.360
Christina: Yes.

00:48:38.360 --> 00:48:42.690
Jordan: So it's much more likely that'll happen
when a higher status female is taking care

00:48:42.690 --> 00:48:43.690
of a lower status infant.

00:48:43.690 --> 00:48:44.690
Christina: Exactly.

00:48:44.690 --> 00:48:46.650
And she said the great tragedy...well, not
tragedy.

00:48:46.650 --> 00:48:49.329
She said the reality of our species...

00:48:49.329 --> 00:48:55.359
And in fact, the subtitle of her book is "That
woman who never evolved.," We didn't evolve

00:48:55.359 --> 00:49:00.119
for niceness and cooperative, there's immense
competition.

00:49:00.119 --> 00:49:06.960
And we can according to her, we are...it's
indelibly, you know, marked in our nature

00:49:06.960 --> 00:49:09.770
to compete for the dominant males.

00:49:09.770 --> 00:49:10.770
Jordan: Oh, yes.

00:49:10.770 --> 00:49:13.390
No doubt about that.

00:49:13.390 --> 00:49:15.230
And that seems true cross-culturally as well.

00:49:15.230 --> 00:49:18.339
That does flatten out a little bit in the
more egalitarian societies.

00:49:18.339 --> 00:49:21.520
So instead of being exaggerated, it does flattened
to some degree.

00:49:21.520 --> 00:49:25.799
So you could imagine that there's a biological
component, and a cultural component.

00:49:25.799 --> 00:49:26.839
Christina: Of course.

00:49:26.839 --> 00:49:27.839
Both.

00:49:27.839 --> 00:49:32.349
Jordan: And in that case, if you modify the
cultural component, then that seems to decrease

00:49:32.349 --> 00:49:33.690
the overall...

00:49:33.690 --> 00:49:37.500
So like, let me be more clear about this.

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:43.570
Women are less prone to mate across and up
status hierarchies in Scandinavia than they

00:49:43.570 --> 00:49:45.549
are in less egalitarian countries.

00:49:45.549 --> 00:49:47.430
But they're still prone to do it.

00:49:47.430 --> 00:49:52.490
So worldwide, for example, young women find
men who are about four years older than them

00:49:52.490 --> 00:49:54.369
maximally attractive.

00:49:54.369 --> 00:49:57.220
And they tend to mate across and up status
hierarchies.

00:49:57.220 --> 00:50:02.420
And so one of the consequences of that, for
example, is that as women have entered the

00:50:02.420 --> 00:50:09.780
workforce, they have actually driven inequality
because rich women will only marry rich men,

00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:14.839
men as rich as them, or richer, whereas rich
men will marry women who are poorer than them.

00:50:14.839 --> 00:50:15.950
But women won't.

00:50:15.950 --> 00:50:20.220
And so what that means is it's another factor
that's pooling wealth in the hands of fewer

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:21.230
and fewer people.

00:50:21.230 --> 00:50:25.070
Christina: It's a sortive of mating now, and
you just find someone with your background,

00:50:25.070 --> 00:50:26.070
your...

00:50:26.070 --> 00:50:29.569
Whereas a doctor might have once married his
secretary, he now marries another doctor.

00:50:29.569 --> 00:50:30.569
Jordan: Yep, yep.

00:50:30.569 --> 00:50:34.421
Danielle: Well, can I ask then, stepping a
little bit back from primates as well, how

00:50:34.421 --> 00:50:41.490
does this selection work in the era of swiping
right and left?

00:50:41.490 --> 00:50:45.290
What is your reaction to the way young people
date today?

00:50:45.290 --> 00:50:46.290
Jordan: Oh, that's a good...

00:50:46.290 --> 00:50:47.910
I was really hoping we'd get into that.

00:50:47.910 --> 00:50:48.910
I was really...

00:50:48.910 --> 00:50:49.910
Because I...

00:50:49.910 --> 00:50:51.650
Danielle: Well, you were very into the monkeys,
so I didn't wanna interrupt that.

00:50:51.650 --> 00:50:52.790
Jordan: No, no...

00:50:52.790 --> 00:50:56.819
Well, I should close off the Scandinavian
discussion just by pointing out, and this

00:50:56.819 --> 00:51:01.160
is something that the Scandinavians are really
gonna have to wrestle with, is that if you

00:51:01.160 --> 00:51:07.819
institute effective policies to promote equality
of opportunity, which the Scandinavians have

00:51:07.819 --> 00:51:10.010
done, you're going to produce some equality.

00:51:10.010 --> 00:51:13.580
So, like a 50/50 distribution of men and women
in the workplace.

00:51:13.580 --> 00:51:18.770
But you're also going to exacerbate certain
kinds of inequality, and you can't get out

00:51:18.770 --> 00:51:19.770
of that.

00:51:19.770 --> 00:51:24.160
So you cannot have equality of opportunity
and equality of outcome together, they don't

00:51:24.160 --> 00:51:25.160
work together.

00:51:25.160 --> 00:51:29.590
And equality of outcome, essentially, quality
of outcome doctrine, which is often described

00:51:29.590 --> 00:51:35.980
with the code word equity, is that at every
level of every occupation, the people have

00:51:35.980 --> 00:51:40.309
to be represented by the same number that
they're represented in at the population.

00:51:40.309 --> 00:51:46.470
So if it's not 50/50 men and women, in each
occupation and in each strata at each occupation,

00:51:46.470 --> 00:51:52.330
then that's sort of prima facie evidence for
systemic discrimination.

00:51:52.330 --> 00:51:55.819
It's like, nope, sorry, you have to factor
in choice.

00:51:55.819 --> 00:51:58.730
And choice actually turns out to be a very
important determinant.

00:51:58.730 --> 00:52:04.970
And as the society gets flatter and flatter,
choice becomes a more and more important determinant.

00:52:04.970 --> 00:52:11.430
And so what that essentially means is that
the most radical end of the left wing political

00:52:11.430 --> 00:52:17.240
agenda is logically impossible, apart from
the fact that it's impossible for a variety

00:52:17.240 --> 00:52:18.240
of other reasons.

00:52:18.240 --> 00:52:19.640
