01:14:36.390 --> 01:14:40.790
Because maybe the right attitude is, well,
you're probably about as flawed as me, and

01:14:40.790 --> 01:14:42.510
you know, we're lucky that we found each other.

01:14:42.510 --> 01:14:46.850
And so let's see if we can make a commitment,
because we're engaging in something that's

01:14:46.850 --> 01:14:49.260
very risky, you know, an intimate relationship.

01:14:49.260 --> 01:14:53.090
And we're gonna commit to each other and see
if we can build something of value across

01:14:53.090 --> 01:14:54.190
time.

01:14:54.190 --> 01:14:57.290
And there's a definite, a risk in that, but
there's a compliment to your partner.

01:14:57.290 --> 01:15:01.440
It's like, well, I think you're worth making
a sacrifice for.

01:15:01.440 --> 01:15:02.440
And what's the sacrifice?

01:15:02.440 --> 01:15:04.140
Well, it's everyone else.

01:15:04.140 --> 01:15:05.870
It's a big sacrifice.

01:15:05.870 --> 01:15:10.450
And if you don't see that as a compliment,
then I don't think you're thinking.

01:15:10.450 --> 01:15:14.110
Because not only is it a compliment, it's
sort of like, the ultimate compliment.

01:15:14.110 --> 01:15:18.390
And maybe you don't get to have a marriage
that works without that compliment.

01:15:18.390 --> 01:15:23.100
Maybe it's so difficult to establish a long-term
relationship that's functional, that you have

01:15:23.100 --> 01:15:27.910
to make a walloping sacrifice very early on
in the relationship in order for that to even

01:15:27.910 --> 01:15:29.090
be a possibility.

01:15:29.090 --> 01:15:33.310
And, you know, maybe not, because what the
hell do we know about what binds people together?

01:15:33.310 --> 01:15:38.070
But it's not that easy to stay with someone
for a long period of time, you know?

01:15:38.070 --> 01:15:39.570
It's a real commitment.

01:15:39.570 --> 01:15:41.930
It takes a tremendous amount of effort.

01:15:41.930 --> 01:15:42.930
So, anyways...

01:15:42.930 --> 01:15:43.930
Danielle: Yeah, but that...

01:15:43.930 --> 01:15:47.290
Actually, you're bringing us back to the beginning,
and your time with Tammy.

01:15:47.290 --> 01:15:50.020
And one of the things about...

01:15:50.020 --> 01:15:54.260
I think, I'm gonna guess this is a bit of
an overlooked part in your...chapter in your

01:15:54.260 --> 01:15:57.200
book, but I just, it was, like, one of my
favorites.

01:15:57.200 --> 01:15:58.460
It was your book on modern parenting.

01:15:58.460 --> 01:15:59.550
Jordan: Oh, yes.

01:15:59.550 --> 01:16:02.060
That's the one I thought I would get in most
trouble for.

01:16:02.060 --> 01:16:03.610
Danielle: I know, but it remind-...

01:16:03.610 --> 01:16:06.120
Jordan: My critics don't read that far into
the book, though.

01:16:06.120 --> 01:16:11.950
Danielle: Well, my mother, when I had my first
child, gave me a 1950s copy of Dr. Spock.

01:16:11.950 --> 01:16:14.360
And he was considered so controversial.

01:16:14.360 --> 01:16:18.870
And yet, he's just, like, the most sensible
person, knew children very, very well, was

01:16:18.870 --> 01:16:19.870
a pediatrician.

01:16:19.870 --> 01:16:25.280
And your rule for parenting is do not let
your children do anything that makes you dislike

01:16:25.280 --> 01:16:26.800
them.

01:16:26.800 --> 01:16:32.360
And you, kind of, in that one chapter, and
it's not even one of your longest chapters,

01:16:32.360 --> 01:16:38.570
just did this wonderful, sweeping overview
of modern parenting and the problems...and

01:16:38.570 --> 01:16:43.750
in some ways, that we're producing, maybe,
some of these kids who are prolonging adulthood,

01:16:43.750 --> 01:16:47.800
prolonging the markers of adulthood.

01:16:47.800 --> 01:16:50.030
That you feel that parents...

01:16:50.030 --> 01:16:55.090
You said you see today's parents as terrified
by their children, not least because they've

01:16:55.090 --> 01:16:59.260
denied credit for their role as benevolent
and necessary agents of discipline, order

01:16:59.260 --> 01:17:00.860
and conventionality.

01:17:00.860 --> 01:17:04.320
And then you told some hilarious stories about
when your wife ran a daycare center out of

01:17:04.320 --> 01:17:05.320
your house.

01:17:05.320 --> 01:17:08.730
And you would get into tests of wills with
some of the two-year-olds, but...

01:17:08.730 --> 01:17:09.730
Jordan: Mm-hmm.

01:17:09.730 --> 01:17:10.730
Yeah, they're tough, man.

01:17:10.730 --> 01:17:11.730
Two-year-olds can be really tough.

01:17:11.730 --> 01:17:12.730
Danielle: Yeah, you described your own son
as ornery.

01:17:12.730 --> 01:17:14.510
I'm glad to hear he got married, congratulations.

01:17:14.510 --> 01:17:15.510
Jordan: Oh, yeah, man.

01:17:15.510 --> 01:17:16.510
Tough, tough kid.

01:17:16.510 --> 01:17:18.620
Yeah, he still doesn't wanna do anything he
doesn't wanna do.

01:17:18.620 --> 01:17:21.440
He's very charming, and very emotionally stable.

01:17:21.440 --> 01:17:25.570
So it's like he's easy to get along with,
but trying to get him to do something he doesn't

01:17:25.570 --> 01:17:26.730
wanna do, it's like...

01:17:26.730 --> 01:17:29.390
He had my wife defeated when he was nine months
old.

01:17:29.390 --> 01:17:30.510
And she's tough.

01:17:30.510 --> 01:17:32.730
Like, seriously, she's no pushover.

01:17:32.730 --> 01:17:35.520
But he would just sit there with his mouth
closed and glare at her.

01:17:35.520 --> 01:17:39.520
It's like, I'm not eating that, and I can
take more than you can dish out.

01:17:39.520 --> 01:17:41.210
It was really something to see, you know?

01:17:41.210 --> 01:17:44.540
To see that kind of force of will in someone
that small.

01:17:44.540 --> 01:17:50.080
Danielle: Talk a little bit about that, and
just the modern roles between men and women.

01:17:50.080 --> 01:17:51.350
I mean, we're less...

01:17:51.350 --> 01:17:55.470
You know, you don't really, you're not really
supposed to distinguish between fathers and

01:17:55.470 --> 01:17:58.740
mothers, even though that seems to inevitably
happen in most...

01:17:58.740 --> 01:18:02.640
Jordan: Well, it happens in large part because
the children differentiate between them.

01:18:02.640 --> 01:18:05.830
Like, parents are under the delusion that
most of what you do with your children is

01:18:05.830 --> 01:18:10.580
driven by what you want to do with your children,
when in fact, it's driven to a massive degree

01:18:10.580 --> 01:18:14.050
by what your children want you to do with
them.

01:18:14.050 --> 01:18:20.310
And so there were studies done 30 years ago
on feminist parents who decided that they

01:18:20.310 --> 01:18:23.570
were going to raise their children in non-gender-differentiated
manners.

01:18:23.570 --> 01:18:28.320
And when they were studied, they found that
the parents who had that explicit philosophy

01:18:28.320 --> 01:18:32.820
were just as gender-differentiated with their
children as the parents who didn't have the

01:18:32.820 --> 01:18:33.820
philosophy.

01:18:33.820 --> 01:18:38.370
And the reason for that is that if you're
a parent that has any sense at all, you don't

01:18:38.370 --> 01:18:43.110
respond to your children as a rigid ideologue,
you respond to them as whatever it is the

01:18:43.110 --> 01:18:46.580
child manifests him or herself as.

01:18:46.580 --> 01:18:52.480
Like, you know, with any individualized relationship,
you take your cue from the person.

01:18:52.480 --> 01:18:55.300
And you might think, well, a child has no
intrinsic nature.

01:18:55.300 --> 01:18:59.610
But you know, if you think that, you either
don't have children, or you've never seen

01:18:59.610 --> 01:19:04.740
a child, or you're so blinded by your ideology
that you don't have a child, you just have

01:19:04.740 --> 01:19:09.190
a blank projection screen onto which you project
your presuppositions.

01:19:09.190 --> 01:19:12.410
And then Heaven help your child, you know?

01:19:12.410 --> 01:19:17.130
So a lot of the gender-differentiation is
actually driven by the children's demands,

01:19:17.130 --> 01:19:19.010
and that's all for the good.

01:19:19.010 --> 01:19:23.360
That chapter, I thought I would get into tremendous
trouble for writing that chapter, because

01:19:23.360 --> 01:19:26.740
it's contentious right on the surface, just
the rule.

01:19:26.740 --> 01:19:30.640
Because the rule first implies that children
can be dis-likeable, and then I would say

01:19:30.640 --> 01:19:33.020
again, you know, it's like...have you met
children?

01:19:33.020 --> 01:19:34.020
Danielle: Yeah, exactly.

01:19:34.020 --> 01:19:35.020
Jordan: Were you ever a child?

01:19:35.020 --> 01:19:36.260
Were there children you didn't like?

01:19:36.260 --> 01:19:37.330
Well, obviously.

01:19:37.330 --> 01:19:41.070
And so lots of children are dis-likeable,
but it's taboo to admit that because they're

01:19:41.070 --> 01:19:43.860
all sweetness and light and innocence, ever
since Rousseau.

01:19:43.860 --> 01:19:48.220
But you know, Rousseau put all five of his
children in an orphanage, where they all died.

01:19:48.220 --> 01:19:50.020
Christina: Oh, he was diabolical.

01:19:50.020 --> 01:19:55.590
Jordan: So maybe we won't talk too much about
Rousseau.

01:19:55.590 --> 01:19:59.521
And then the next taboo is, well, that parents
can dislike their children, but if you're

01:19:59.521 --> 01:20:05.600
a clinician and you don't think that parents
can dislike their children, well then, you're

01:20:05.600 --> 01:20:06.690
not a clinician.

01:20:06.690 --> 01:20:13.280
Because one of the things you constantly see
is that pathology within families is an incredibly

01:20:13.280 --> 01:20:17.640
common source of psychological destabilization,
right?

01:20:17.640 --> 01:20:22.100
And it's terrible tension between parents
and their children, and between siblings.

01:20:22.100 --> 01:20:27.600
What I suggest in the in the book, which I
think is radical by today's standards, is

01:20:27.600 --> 01:20:34.800
that your fundamental job as a parent is to
ensure that by the time your child is four

01:20:34.800 --> 01:20:41.190
years old, that they are maximally desirable
to other children and to adults.

01:20:41.190 --> 01:20:48.590
Because what happens is that after the age
of four, you aren't the primary agent of socialization,

01:20:48.590 --> 01:20:51.910
the social world becomes the primary agent
of socialization.

01:20:51.910 --> 01:20:57.680
And if your child is the sort of child that's
invited to play by other children, because

01:20:57.680 --> 01:21:02.890
your child is capable of forestalling gratification,
and taking turns, and playing someone else's

01:21:02.890 --> 01:21:08.030
game when it's necessary, and abiding by the
rules, and not having a temper tantrum when

01:21:08.030 --> 01:21:13.820
they lose, and not getting too, you know,
high on their horse when they win, then many

01:21:13.820 --> 01:21:15.320
children will invite them.

01:21:15.320 --> 01:21:17.030
Danielle: Sorry, I wasn't...I was doing false signals...

01:21:17.030 --> 01:21:19.890
Christina: But we have all these signals,
I don't know what you mean.

01:21:19.890 --> 01:21:20.890
Just say it.

01:21:20.890 --> 01:21:21.890
What do you want me to do.

01:21:21.890 --> 01:21:24.540
Danielle: No, I know our limited time, and
so I just wanted to...

01:21:24.540 --> 01:21:28.420
I was actually saying do you have any more
questions?

01:21:28.420 --> 01:21:29.420
Jordan: Any more questions?

01:21:29.420 --> 01:21:30.620
Danielle: But this is great.

01:21:30.620 --> 01:21:33.220
It was selfish of me, because I really wanted
to hear you talk about parenting.

01:21:33.220 --> 01:21:34.220
Jordan: Well, I'll follow...

01:21:34.220 --> 01:21:35.360
Christina: I know, I just wanna let him, like,
take...

01:21:35.360 --> 01:21:37.830
Jordan: I'll take 15 seconds to wrap that
up, and then stop.

01:21:37.830 --> 01:21:38.830
Danielle: Yes, please.

01:21:38.830 --> 01:21:39.830
Jordan: Well, so look.

01:21:39.830 --> 01:21:44.310
You get married when you have your children,
and you're flawed, and your partner's flawed.

01:21:44.310 --> 01:21:45.910
And hopefully you're flawed in different ways.

01:21:45.910 --> 01:21:51.220
And so you put the two of you together, and
you make one approximately normal person.

01:21:51.220 --> 01:21:52.220
And then...

01:21:52.220 --> 01:21:53.220
Hopefully.

01:21:53.220 --> 01:21:58.250
And then your child has to interact with that
dyad that is a reasonable representative of

01:21:58.250 --> 01:22:00.240
social norms.

01:22:00.240 --> 01:22:06.870
And if your child disappoints you with their
behavior, the probability that they will disappoint

01:22:06.870 --> 01:22:08.960
other people is very high.

01:22:08.960 --> 01:22:14.570
And so you have an ethical obligation to ensure
that your child is behaving in a manner that

01:22:14.570 --> 01:22:19.870
makes them optimally desirable to their playmates,
and also to other adults.

01:22:19.870 --> 01:22:25.430
Because then, the kids invite them to play
and they get to be socialized, right?

01:22:25.430 --> 01:22:27.220
They have friends, for God's sake.

01:22:27.220 --> 01:22:28.520
It's like, what do you want for your kids?

01:22:28.520 --> 01:22:29.980
How about some friends?

01:22:29.980 --> 01:22:31.100
Wouldn't that be nice?

01:22:31.100 --> 01:22:35.000
And maybe what you'd like is that they regulate
their behavior well enough so that when you

01:22:35.000 --> 01:22:39.781
take them places, restaurants, to see your
friends, to see your relatives, they behave

01:22:39.781 --> 01:22:45.920
in a manner that's sufficiently civilized
so their intrinsic charm wins over the adults,

01:22:45.920 --> 01:22:50.950
and everywhere they go people are smiling
and welcoming, instead of wishing, with fake

01:22:50.950 --> 01:22:55.080
smiles, that the damn brat would leave, along
with their foolish parents.

01:22:55.080 --> 01:23:01.760
Which is not a good...that's not a good environment
to have your child constantly exposed to.

01:23:01.760 --> 01:23:04.740
No friends, because they're too selfish and
immature.

01:23:04.740 --> 01:23:11.290
And irritating to adults, so that they're
barely tolerated under the mask of false smiles.

01:23:11.290 --> 01:23:15.941
It's like, you have an ethical obligation
to regulate your child's behavior so that

01:23:15.941 --> 01:23:18.040
they're optimally acceptable socially.

01:23:18.040 --> 01:23:21.180
And that is not how people look at children
in the modern world.

01:23:21.180 --> 01:23:24.980
They think, well, you're raising their self
esteem, or you're enhancing their creativity.

01:23:24.980 --> 01:23:28.360
Or you don't wanna put constraints on their
behavior because you're going to interfere

01:23:28.360 --> 01:23:31.070
with the flowering of their intrinsic self.

01:23:31.070 --> 01:23:37.130
And you know, it's all Rousseau-ian nonsense,
and there's no evidence to support it.

01:23:37.130 --> 01:23:38.130
Danielle: And he's no expert.

01:23:38.130 --> 01:23:39.130
Jordan: And he's no expert.

01:23:39.130 --> 01:23:40.130
That's right.

01:23:40.130 --> 01:23:41.300
Christina: Oh, my God, he was the worst father
in history.

01:23:41.300 --> 01:23:42.300
Jordan: Yeah.

01:23:42.300 --> 01:23:43.300
Yeah.

01:23:43.300 --> 01:23:44.300
Christina: And such a corrupt...

01:23:44.300 --> 01:23:50.080
And he had these babies with this poor scullery
maid, and left them all in a...actually, a

01:23:50.080 --> 01:23:52.630
place where they would just languish and die.

01:23:52.630 --> 01:23:53.630
Jordan: Yeah, right.

01:23:53.630 --> 01:23:54.630
Five of them.

01:23:54.630 --> 01:23:55.630
Christina: Rousseau.

01:23:55.630 --> 01:23:56.630
Jordan: Yeah, I know.

01:23:56.630 --> 01:23:57.630
I know.

01:23:57.630 --> 01:23:58.630
Yeah, exactly.

01:23:58.630 --> 01:23:59.630
Man is intrinsically good.

01:23:59.630 --> 01:24:00.630
Yeah, well, except for Jean-Jacques Rousseau.

01:24:00.630 --> 01:24:05.950
Christina: Yeah, well I just remember a few
weeks ago I was reading about you, and somehow

01:24:05.950 --> 01:24:11.150
I got onto somebody's Twitter feed, whom I
will not mention, because oh, my God.

01:24:11.150 --> 01:24:13.380
But anyway, a difficult person.

01:24:13.380 --> 01:24:21.350
And she was attacking you, and had a selection
from your book where you had called two-year-olds

01:24:21.350 --> 01:24:22.690
little monsters.

01:24:22.690 --> 01:24:29.830
And so suddenly, all of these distraught Twitter
followers of this feminist were saying "He

01:24:29.830 --> 01:24:31.940
called them monsters?" you know?

01:24:31.940 --> 01:24:33.010
Jordan: Little monsters.

01:24:33.010 --> 01:24:34.130
Christina: Yeah, little monsters.

01:24:34.130 --> 01:24:40.380
And everybody...and then occasionally there'd
be a parent who would say, "Two-year-olds

01:24:40.380 --> 01:24:41.940
kind of are monsters," you know?

01:24:41.940 --> 01:24:44.420
And then there'd be "Agghhhh!!!" and
then, "They kind of are..."

01:24:44.420 --> 01:24:49.300
And they had taken this out of context and
shown it like something to deplore.

01:24:49.300 --> 01:24:51.900
And it was so amusing to me, this little game...

01:24:51.900 --> 01:24:52.900
Jordan: Mm-hmm, mm-hmm.

01:24:52.900 --> 01:24:54.330
Hopefully they'll soon be cursed the some
two-year-olds.

01:24:54.330 --> 01:24:55.330
Danielle: Right, right.

01:24:55.330 --> 01:24:57.130
Christina: They're gonna get their own little
monsters.

01:24:57.130 --> 01:25:00.690
My son, I won't say which
one, was two years old and had...he was a

01:25:00.690 --> 01:25:01.690
good boy.

01:25:01.690 --> 01:25:05.140
But he had an insane meltdown in a supermarket.

01:25:05.140 --> 01:25:08.750
I was with my mother, we both pretended we
didn't know him.

01:25:08.750 --> 01:25:10.770
We didn't wanna be the parent, you know?

01:25:10.770 --> 01:25:11.770
Jordan: Oh, yeah.

01:25:11.770 --> 01:25:13.020
Oh, yeah.

01:25:13.020 --> 01:25:14.760
Christina: And we heard people say, "Oh, my
God.

01:25:14.770 --> 01:25:15.770
Look at that child "Oh, oh my god!"

01:25:15.770 --> 01:25:16.770
Jordan: Oh, have...

01:25:16.770 --> 01:25:18.460
Watching a two-year-old have a tantrum is...it's
a real little miracle.

01:25:18.460 --> 01:25:19.460
Christina: It was terrifying.

01:25:19.460 --> 01:25:20.460
I didn't wanna be associated with him.

01:25:20.460 --> 01:25:24.000
Jordan: We had a boy who used to be in...like,
when my wife was taking care of more kids

01:25:24.000 --> 01:25:30.200
than ours, there was a little boy who had
learned to throw a pretty decent tantrum.

01:25:30.200 --> 01:25:33.830
And he would do that, and it didn't work in
our house because we'd just leave him.

01:25:33.830 --> 01:25:35.750
Have his tantrum, and go into a different
room.

01:25:35.750 --> 01:25:38.540
And then he'd kind of wake up out of it, and
there wouldn't be anybody around.

01:25:38.540 --> 01:25:39.540
And so that...

01:25:39.540 --> 01:25:43.730
Like, if you put all that work into a dramatic
display, and you have zero audience, you're

01:25:43.730 --> 01:25:45.400
not gonna sustain it.

01:25:45.400 --> 01:25:49.810
Anyways, he could actually hold his breath
until he turned blue.

01:25:49.810 --> 01:25:51.080
So you should try that.

01:25:51.080 --> 01:25:53.380
Go home and see if you can do that in front
of the mirror, man.

01:25:53.380 --> 01:25:54.660
Like, it's hard.

01:25:54.660 --> 01:25:55.890
It's very, very...

01:25:55.890 --> 01:25:56.890
You have to be...

01:25:56.890 --> 01:25:57.890
Christina: You gotta admire it.

01:25:57.890 --> 01:25:58.890
Jordan: You do.

01:25:58.890 --> 01:25:59.890
It was impressive.

01:25:59.890 --> 01:26:01.540
And you know, two-year-olds are very impressive.

01:26:01.540 --> 01:26:05.910
They have unbelievable outbursts of rage,
and dis-inhibited emotion.

01:26:05.910 --> 01:26:11.251
And your job is, you know, they're driven
by these underlying motivational systems that

01:26:11.251 --> 01:26:12.710
are unbelievably powerful.

01:26:12.710 --> 01:26:17.260
And it's part of what makes them delightful,
because when they're happy, they're insanely

01:26:17.260 --> 01:26:18.260
happy.

01:26:18.260 --> 01:26:19.670
And when they're playful, they're incredibly
playful.

01:26:19.670 --> 01:26:25.440
And so the positive end of them is way exaggerated
compared to, you know, a rather drab adult.

01:26:25.440 --> 01:26:28.570
And so it makes two-year-olds extraordinarily
interesting.

01:26:28.570 --> 01:26:32.750
But the same is true on the negative emotion
side, they're completely dis-regulated, and

01:26:32.750 --> 01:26:34.070
it's really hard on them.

01:26:34.070 --> 01:26:37.931
Like, to have a two-year-old who isn't in
control of their emotions means that you have

01:26:37.931 --> 01:26:41.710
a child who's developing central personality.

01:26:41.710 --> 01:26:46.300
You know, their ego, for lack of a better
word, is constantly being swamped by these

01:26:46.300 --> 01:26:48.770
powerful underlying emotional systems.

01:26:48.770 --> 01:26:49.770
You know, what it's like.

01:26:49.770 --> 01:26:53.891
If you are enraged for any period of time,
or if you're engulfed by grief, like, it's

01:26:53.891 --> 01:26:56.020
exhausting.

01:26:56.020 --> 01:26:57.930
It's demeaning, and it's exhausting.

01:26:57.930 --> 01:26:59.970
And it's the same with a little kid.

01:26:59.970 --> 01:27:05.970
It's like, it's a real defeat for the developing
integrated individual to be subjugated by

01:27:05.970 --> 01:27:09.590
those catastrophically powerful emergent emotions.

01:27:09.590 --> 01:27:15.830
And part of your job as a parent is to scaffold
the part of the child that can regulate and

01:27:15.830 --> 01:27:18.270
inhibit those powerful underlying systems.

01:27:18.270 --> 01:27:23.010
So with my son, for example, when he used
to misbehave, I would count and say, "You're

01:27:23.010 --> 01:27:24.290
gonna go sit on the steps."

01:27:24.290 --> 01:27:26.170
He'd say, "Oh, no, I'm not."

01:27:26.170 --> 01:27:28.340
I would say, "Oh, yes, you are."

01:27:28.340 --> 01:27:31.840
And then usually I'd have to chase him around,
because he wouldn't go sit on the steps.

01:27:31.840 --> 01:27:35.220
And so I'd put him on the steps and say, "You're
gonna sit there until you've got yourself

01:27:35.220 --> 01:27:37.050
under control."

01:27:37.050 --> 01:27:40.400
And so he'd say, "No, I'm not," and I'd say,
"Yes, you are, " and then he'd try to get

01:27:40.400 --> 01:27:42.870
up, and I'd just hold him and say, "You're
gonna sit there.

01:27:42.870 --> 01:27:44.820
I'm gonna hold you 'til you sit there."

01:27:44.820 --> 01:27:46.120
"No, I'm not."

01:27:46.120 --> 01:27:49.370
It's like, I could out-wait a two-year-old.

01:27:49.370 --> 01:27:53.030
So I usually won those battles, and then he'd
sit there, and I'd say, "Look, kid, this is

01:27:53.030 --> 01:27:54.150
the deal," I'd say two things.

01:27:54.150 --> 01:27:56.280
"Like, do you wanna have a bad day, or do
you wanna have a good day?

01:27:56.280 --> 01:27:57.280
You think about that.

01:27:57.280 --> 01:27:58.350
Because if you wanna have a good day, we can
just have a bad day."

01:27:58.350 --> 01:27:59.960
Danielle: A little Clint Eastwood of you with
them.

01:27:59.960 --> 01:28:02.720
Jordan: "But if you wanna have a bad day,
we can have a bad day.

01:28:02.720 --> 01:28:07.460
So you sit here, and as soon as you can control
yourself and you ready to be civilized, then

01:28:07.460 --> 01:28:09.780
you can come back and we can have a good day."

01:28:09.780 --> 01:28:17.110
So he'd sit there, just, just...it was unbelievable
to watch, Just enveloped with rage, you know?

01:28:17.110 --> 01:28:20.240
Just argh, trying to get himself under control,
you know?

01:28:20.240 --> 01:28:26.060
And so I'd come back 30 seconds later, and
I'd say, "You know, have you got yourself,

01:28:26.060 --> 01:28:28.200
got your act together yet?"

01:28:28.200 --> 01:28:29.200
"No!

01:28:29.200 --> 01:28:30.200
Not yet!"

01:28:30.200 --> 01:28:34.310
And So I'd wait, and usually it took him two
or three minutes, and he'd calm down.

01:28:34.310 --> 01:28:37.780
And then he'd come back out and he'd say,
"I'm ready to have a good day," and he meant

01:28:37.780 --> 01:28:38.780
it, you know?

01:28:38.780 --> 01:28:42.960
And I could tell he meant it, too, because
whatever resentment I was harboring towards

01:28:42.960 --> 01:28:47.960
him for his misbehavior, and you have to watch
that when you're an adult, would vanish because

01:28:47.960 --> 01:28:50.580
he'd come, and he was done.

01:28:50.580 --> 01:28:54.100
He was ready, just, you know, to proceed on
a civilized basis.

01:28:54.100 --> 01:28:59.560
And it was really interesting to watch that
because it took him...every time he sat on

01:28:59.560 --> 01:29:03.070
the steps, it took him a shorter and shorter
period of time to attain mastery, right?

01:29:03.070 --> 01:29:07.710
Until, you know, it got to the point where
he could only have to sit for 15 seconds or

01:29:07.710 --> 01:29:10.800
so, and he would bring himself under control.

01:29:10.800 --> 01:29:12.150
And that was a victory.

01:29:12.150 --> 01:29:17.170
Like, if you imagine the neurological systems
develop that are responsible for personality

01:29:17.170 --> 01:29:21.440
integration, it was a victory for those systems
because they were attaining the ability to

01:29:21.440 --> 01:29:25.860
regulate the lower order spontaneous emotions.

01:29:25.860 --> 01:29:30.820
And you know, he turned into an individual
who's capable of a tremendous level of self-control.

01:29:30.820 --> 01:29:33.830
And you know, and he had large demons to fight
with.

01:29:33.830 --> 01:29:34.940
Woman: Good for his wife.

01:29:34.940 --> 01:29:36.060
Thank God for his wife.

01:29:36.060 --> 01:29:37.060
Jordan: Yes, absolutely.

01:29:37.060 --> 01:29:40.290
And well, it turned out well for my daughter,
too, because she ended up being very ill,

01:29:40.290 --> 01:29:45.750
and he ended up being extraordinarily level-headed
and reliable, and thank God for that.

